window.SOURCE_TEXT = "SOURCE OF TRUTH — Chapter 1, as supplied by the author on 2026-10-31.\nNothing in the site may teach a fact, a number, a term or an Arabic translation\nthat is not on this page. Provenance is checked against this file.\n\n================================================================================\nHOW TO USE THIS BOOK — front matter, as supplied\n================================================================================\n\nThis book covers every Learning Outcome Statement in CMA Part 1, Section A\n(External Financial Reporting Decisions), effective September 1, 2024. It is\nwritten in graded English (B1+ to B2) with the exact exam terms, and it bridges\neach term to Arabic and French.\n\nFrom the September/October 2026 testing window, English-language CMA exams have\n100 multiple-choice questions followed by two case-based questions (CBQs). The\nLearning Outcome Statements did not change. Every chapter therefore ends with\nmultiple-choice practice and a case-style set in the CBQ format.\n\nFour parts: Part I, Chapters 1 to 5, sets up the language of accounting and the\nfour financial statements. Part II, Chapters 6 to 10, measures assets. Part III,\nChapters 11 to 15, covers revenue, liabilities, income taxes, leases and income\nmeasurement. Part IV, Chapters 16 to 18, covers consolidation, the six tested\ndifferences from IFRS, and integrated reporting.\n\nEach chapter follows the same pattern: an opener figure, learning objectives with\nLOS codes, key terms, numbered sections, a section check after each section, a\nchapter summary figure, practice questions, a case question in the official exam\nformat and full answers with explanations.\n\nCase companies. Orontes Foods, Inc. is a U.S. GAAP food and beverage producer\nbased in Amman, Jordan, listed in the United States; its 2025 statements are\nbuilt in Chapters 2 to 5 and reused later. Barada Wholesale, Inc. is a small\nU.S. GAAP distributor used for the case-style sets. Cedar Retail S.A.L. is the\nIFRS company in Chapter 1.\n\nNumbers. Amounts are in whole U.S. dollars unless a table says USD 000. Examples\nthat begin with 'Suppose', and all dated examples after 2025, are illustrative:\nthey are not part of Orontes's 2025 statements. Figure and question numbers keep\ntheir chapter prefix, for example F11-03 or P11-07.\n\nTHE BOXES IN EACH CHAPTER\nWhat you already know | Links the topic to IFRS or to earlier chapters.\nWorked example | A calculation or analysis, step by step.\nYour turn | A faded exercise: the first lines are done; you complete the rest.\n  Answers at the end of the chapter.\nExam trap | A common wrong answer and why it is wrong.\nFalse friend | An Arabic or French term that looks right but means something else\n  in U.S. GAAP.\nIFRS | How IFRS differs, for readers who trained in IFRS.\nWatch: not yet testable | Issued or proposed changes that the exam does not test yet.\nKey words in this section | English exam term with its Arabic and French equivalents.\nTest yourself | Section check questions. Answers at the end of the chapter.\n\nPRACTICE — the author's preamble\nAnswer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case question: like the\nexam, it gives a short case and an exhibit, followed by independent items\n(drag-and-drop, select from a list, fill in the blank and calculations). The\nanswers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are\nright.\n\n================================================================================\nCHAPTER 1 — The Language and Framework of Financial Reporting\nلغة التقارير المالية وإطارها\n================================================================================\n\nFinancial reporting has its own language. You already know many of its ideas from\nyour accounting studies in Arabic or French. In this chapter you meet the English\nwords that the CMA exam uses for those ideas, and you see how U.S. rules organize\nthem.\n\nWe start with the people who read financial statements. Then we look at the\nbuilding blocks of the statements, the accounting equation, double entry and the\naccrual basis. We finish with the rule makers and a first look at the four\nstatements. Figure F01-01 is your map.\n\nFIGURE F01-01. Roadmap of Chapter 1: seven linked topics from users to the four\nfinancial statements.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLEARNING OBJECTIVES\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLOS | Official statement (ICMA, 2024) | In plain words | Depth here\n\nA.1.a | identify the users of these financial statements and their needs |\n  Identify the users of financial statements and what each user needs. |\n  Full treatment\n\nA.1.b | demonstrate an understanding of the purposes and uses of each statement |\n  State what each of the four financial statements is for. |\n  Overview (full in Chapters 2–5)\n\nA.1.e | identify how various financial transactions affect the elements of each of\n  the financial statements and determine the proper classification of a given\n  transaction | Show how a transaction changes the accounting equation, and\n  classify simple transactions. | Introduction (full in Chapters 2–5)\n\nA.2.z | demonstrate an understanding of the matching principle with respect to\n  revenues and expenses, and be able to apply it to a specific situation |\n  Explain the accrual basis and the matching principle. |\n  Introduction (full in Chapter 11)\n\nBackground | Background for this book (not an ICMA learning outcome). | Describe\n  who sets U.S. GAAP and IFRS, and which rules the CMA exam uses. | Background\n\nThe official statements are quoted from the ICMA Learning Outcome Statements\n(effective September 1, 2024). The plain-words column restates them in simpler\nEnglish. The exam may test any outcome up to Level C.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKEY TERMS IN THIS CHAPTER (16 rows)\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nfinancial statements | القوائم المالية\nprimary users | المستخدمون الرئيسيون\nasset | أصل (أصول)\nliability | التزام (التزامات)\nequity | حقوق الملكية\nrevenue | إيراد (إيرادات)\nexpense | مصروف (مصروفات)\nretained earnings | الأرباح المحتجزة\ndividend | توزيعات الأرباح\naccounting equation | المعادلة المحاسبية\ndebit | مدين\ncredit | دائن\naccrual basis | أساس الاستحقاق\nmatching principle | مبدأ مقابلة الإيرادات بالمصروفات\nU.S. GAAP | المبادئ المحاسبية المقبولة عموماً في الولايات المتحدة\nIFRS | المعايير الدولية لإعداد التقارير المالية\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBOX — WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nYou have studied accounting before, perhaps in Arabic or French. The ideas in this\nchapter are not new to you: assets and liabilities, debit and credit, the trial\nbalance. Three things are new.\n• The English names. Some are close to your language and some are not. The\n  False-Friend boxes warn you about words that look familiar but mean something else.\n• The U.S. rules. The CMA exam uses U.S. GAAP. If you learned IFRS, most ideas are\n  the same, but some names and some rules differ. The IFRS Contrast boxes show where.\n• The exam wording. CMA questions use exact terms, and one word can change the\n  answer. The Language Focus boxes help you read questions carefully.\n\n================================================================================\n1.1  Who uses financial statements, and why?\n================================================================================\n\nA company collects thousands of facts every day: sales, purchases, salaries and\nloans. Financial statements summarize these facts in a standard form for people\noutside the company. The four main statements are the balance sheet, the income\nstatement, the statement of changes in equity and the statement of cash flows.\n\nMost companies prepare general-purpose financial statements. These are reports for\nmany readers at the same time, not for one special reader. The U.S. conceptual\nframework names the most important readers. The primary users are existing and\npotential investors, lenders and other creditors. They give resources (money, goods\nor credit) to the company. They usually cannot ask the company for special reports,\nso they depend on the published statements.\n\nEach user makes a different decision, so each user looks for different information.\n\nFIGURE F01-02. Users of financial statements and what they need\nUser | Decision | Key question | Statements used most\nInvestors (shareholders) — PRIMARY | Buy, hold or sell shares | Will the company\n  create future cash flows and returns? | Income statement, cash flows, balance sheet\nLenders (banks, bondholders) — PRIMARY | Lend, renew or stop a loan | Can the\n  company pay interest and repay the principal on time? | Cash flows, balance sheet\nOther creditors (suppliers) — PRIMARY | Sell on credit, set credit terms | Will the\n  company pay its bills when due? | Balance sheet (short-term items), cash flows\nEmployees and unions | Stay, negotiate pay | Is the company stable and profitable? |\n  Income statement\nCustomers | Sign long-term contracts | Will the company continue to supply us? |\n  Balance sheet, income statement\nGovernments and regulators | Tax, oversight, statistics | Does the company follow\n  the rules and pay its taxes? | All statements and notes\nManagers (internal) | Plan and control operations | Not a primary user: can use\n  internal reports | Internal management reports\n\nOther groups also read financial statements: employees, customers, governments, tax\nauthorities and the public. Managers read them too. However, managers are not\nprimary users of general-purpose statements, because they can get any internal\ninformation they need. Reporting for managers is management accounting, the subject\nof most of the CMA exam.\n\nWhat do all these users need? In simple terms, they ask three questions.\n• What does the company have, and what does it owe? The balance sheet answers this.\n• How did the company perform in the period? The income statement answers this.\n• Where did cash come from, and where did it go? The statement of cash flows answers this.\n\nUsers also want to judge stewardship: how well the managers used the owners' resources.\n\nWHAT MAKES INFORMATION USEFUL?\nThe conceptual framework lists the qualities of useful information. There are two\nfundamental qualities. Relevance means the information can make a difference to a\ndecision. It helps users predict the future (predictive value) or check their past\npredictions (confirmatory value). Relevance includes materiality: an item is\nmaterial if leaving it out or stating it wrongly could change users' decisions.\n\nFaithful representation means the information shows what it claims to show. It is\ncomplete, neutral and free from error. Four enhancing qualities make information\neven more useful: comparability, verifiability, timeliness and understandability.\nFinally, there is a cost constraint: the benefit of reporting information should be\ngreater than its cost.\n\nBOX — EXAM TRAP: Who is a primary user?\nPrimary users are investors, lenders and other creditors. Managers are users, but\nnot primary users of general-purpose statements. Also remember: relevance and\nfaithful representation are the two fundamental qualities. Comparability,\nverifiability, timeliness and understandability are only enhancing qualities.\n\nBOX — LANGUAGE FOCUS: Five accounting verbs\nThese verbs have exact meanings in exam questions. Learn the difference.\nrecognize | include an item in the statements, with a name and an amount\nmeasure   | decide the amount of the item\nrecord    | enter the item in the accounts (journal and ledger)\npresent   | show the item on the face of a statement\ndisclose  | explain the item in the notes to the statements\nExample: Orontes recognizes revenue when it delivers the goods. It measures the\nrevenue at the invoice price. It presents revenue in the income statement, and it\ndiscloses its revenue policy in the notes.\n\nBOX — TERM BRIDGE: Key words in this section (8 rows)\nfinancial statements | القوائم المالية\ngeneral-purpose financial statements | القوائم المالية ذات الغرض العام\nprimary users | المستخدمون الرئيسيون\nrelevance | الملاءمة\nfaithful representation | التمثيل الصادق\nmateriality | الأهمية النسبية\nrecognize | يعترف (الاعتراف)\ndisclose | يُفصح (الإفصاح)\n\nSECTION CHECK\nSC1-1  Which group is a primary user of general-purpose financial statements?\n  A. The company's managers\n  B. The company's internal auditors\n  C. The company's board of directors\n  D. Existing and potential lenders\nSC1-2  Information helps an investor forecast next year's cash flows. This\n       information has:\n  A. predictive value\n  B. verifiability\n  C. comparability\n  D. timeliness\n\n================================================================================\n1.2  The building blocks: elements and the accounting equation\n================================================================================\n\nEvery amount in the financial statements belongs to an element. The FASB conceptual\nframework defines ten elements. Three of them describe the balance sheet at one date.\n• An asset is a present right of an entity to an economic benefit. Examples: cash,\n  accounts receivable, inventory and equipment.\n• A liability is a present obligation of an entity to transfer an economic benefit.\n  Examples: accounts payable, notes payable and wages payable.\n• Equity is the residual interest in the assets after the liabilities are deducted.\n  It is the owners' claim.\n\nThe other elements describe changes during a period.\n• Revenues and expenses come from the company's central, ongoing operations. When\n  Orontes sells olive oil, it earns revenue. When it pays plant workers, it has an expense.\n• Gains and losses come from peripheral or incidental events. If Orontes sells an old\n  delivery truck for more than its carrying amount, it has a gain.\n• Investments by owners, such as buying new shares, increase equity. Distributions to\n  owners, such as dividends, decrease equity.\n• Comprehensive income is the total change in equity from sources other than owners.\n  You will meet it in Chapters 3 and 15.\n\nThe accounting equation connects the three balance sheet elements:\n    Assets = Liabilities + Equity\nThe equation always holds, because creditors (liabilities) or owners (equity) finance\nevery asset. Equity has two main parts. Contributed capital is what owners paid in:\ncommon stock at its par value, plus additional paid-in capital (APIC). Retained\nearnings (RE) are the past net income that the company kept instead of paying it out\nas dividends.\n\nFIGURE F01-03. The accounting equation A = L + E, with equity split into contributed\ncapital and retained earnings.\n\nLook at the lower part of Figure F01-03. Revenues and gains increase retained\nearnings. Expenses and losses decrease it. Dividends also decrease it, but a dividend\nis not an expense. It is a distribution to owners, so it never appears in the income\nstatement.\n\nBOX — IFRS CONTRAST: Same idea, different words\nThe IFRS Conceptual Framework (2018) defines an asset as a present economic resource\ncontrolled by the entity as a result of past events. The idea is the same as in U.S.\nGAAP; only the wording is different. IFRS also uses the word income for both revenue\nand gains, while U.S. GAAP keeps revenues and gains apart. You may also know these\nIFRS names: statement of financial position (balance sheet), share capital (common\nstock) and share premium (additional paid-in capital).\n\nBOX — FALSE-FRIEND ALERT: Words that look familiar\n• stock — In U.S. English, stock means shares: common stock, a stock dividend. In\n  French and British English, stock often means inventory. In a CMA question,\n  inventory is always inventory.\n• income and revenue — Arabic دخل is not إيراد. Revenue is the gross amount from\n  sales. Net income is what remains after expenses. Do not use income when the\n  question means revenue.\n• résultat and charges — In French, résultat is the profit or loss (net income), and\n  charges are expenses. In English, charges usually means fees.\n• balance — In French, la balance is the trial balance. The balance sheet is le bilan.\n\nBOX — TERM BRIDGE: Key words in this section (14 rows)\nasset | أصل (أصول)\nliability | التزام (التزامات)\nequity | حقوق الملكية\nrevenue | إيراد (إيرادات)\nexpense | مصروف (مصروفات)\ngain | مكسب (مكاسب)\nloss | خسارة\nnet income | صافي الدخل\nretained earnings | الأرباح المحتجزة\ncommon stock | الأسهم العادية\nadditional paid-in capital | علاوة إصدار الأسهم (رأس المال الإضافي المدفوع)\npar value | القيمة الاسمية\ndividend | توزيعات الأرباح\naccounting equation | المعادلة المحاسبية\n\nSECTION CHECK\nSC2-1  Which item is an expense?\n  A. A cash dividend paid to shareholders\n  B. Wages paid to plant workers\n  C. Repayment of a bank loan\n  D. Purchase of a new bottling line\nSC2-2  A company has total assets of 500 and total liabilities of 320. What is its equity?\n  A. 180   B. 320   C. 500   D. 820\n\n================================================================================\n1.3  Double entry: debits and credits\n================================================================================\n\nAccountants record every transaction with the double-entry system. Each transaction\naffects at least two accounts, and total debits always equal total credits. This is\nwhy the accounting equation always balances.\n\nDebit simply means the left side of an account, and credit means the right side.\nNeither word means good or bad, and neither word always means increase. Whether a\ndebit increases an account depends on the type of account. Each account has a normal\nbalance: the side that increases it.\n\nFIGURE F01-04. Debit and credit rules: which six account types increase with a debit\nand which with a credit.\n\nThe expanded equation at the bottom of Figure F01-04 is a useful memory aid. Accounts\non the left side of the equation (assets, expenses and dividends) increase with a\ndebit. Accounts on the right side (liabilities, equity and revenues) increase with a\ncredit.\n\nA contra account reduces a related account, so it has the opposite normal balance.\nFor example, accumulated depreciation reduces equipment. It has a credit balance, but\nit belongs with the assets. The allowance for credit losses reduces accounts\nreceivable in the same way (Chapter 6).\n\nBOX — WORKED EXAMPLE: Orontes Foods in January\nOrontes Foods, Inc. makes olive oil and tahini in Amman, Jordan, and frozen pastries\nin Dubai. Its shares are listed on a U.S. stock exchange, and it reports under U.S.\nGAAP. Here are its journal entries for six transactions in January 2025. Amounts are\nin USD 000.\n\n1 | Jan 2  | Cash 1,500 / Common stock 100, Additional paid-in capital 1,400\n            Issued 100,000 new shares of $1 par common stock for $15 per share, in cash.\n2 | Jan 5  | Cash 800 / Notes payable (long-term) 800\n            Borrowed cash from Levant Commerce Bank on a 5-year note at 6% interest.\n3 | Jan 10 | Equipment 1,200 / Cash 1,200\n            Bought a new bottling line for the Amman plant and paid cash.\n4 | Jan 18 | Accounts receivable 300 / Sales revenue 300\n            Cost of goods sold 180 / Inventory 180\n            Sold olive oil to GreenBasket Supermarkets on credit. The goods had cost\n            Orontes less than the selling price.\n5 | Jan 31 | Wages expense 40 / Cash 40\n            Paid January wages to plant workers in cash.\n6 | Jan 31 | Retained earnings (dividends declared) 50 / Dividends payable 50\n            The board declared a cash dividend, payable on February 15.\nTotals 4,070 = 4,070\n\nCheck each entry: the debits equal the credits.\n\nFIGURE F01-05. Effect grid for six Orontes Foods transactions in January 2025 (USD 000).\n\nRead the grid row by row. Three points matter for the exam.\n• Rows 1 and 2 bring in cash of 2,300 in total, but no revenue. Money from owners and\n  lenders is financing. It is not income.\n• Row 4 creates revenue of 300 but no cash. Orontes records revenue when it delivers\n  the goods, not when the customer pays.\n• Row 6 reduces retained earnings by 50 and creates a liability. No cash moves until\n  February, and net income does not change.\n\nThe totals prove the equation. Assets rose by 2,380, liabilities rose by 850 and\nequity rose by 1,530.\n\nBOX — EXAM TRAP: Three classic mistakes\n• A dividend is not an expense. It reduces retained earnings, not net income.\n• Cash from borrowing or from issuing shares is not revenue. It is financing.\n• Buying equipment for cash is not an expense today. One asset (cash) becomes another\n  asset (equipment). The cost becomes an expense later, through depreciation.\n\nBOX — YOUR TURN: Orontes Foods in February\nHere are six transactions from February (USD 000).\n1. Feb 3:  Paid a supplier for olives bought on credit in January. Amount: 150.\n2. Feb 8:  Collected part of the January receivable from GreenBasket Supermarkets. Amount: 200.\n3. Feb 10: Paid 12 months of warehouse rent in advance. Amount: 24.\n4. Feb 12: A Dubai hotel group paid cash in advance for pastries that Orontes will\n           deliver in March. Amount: 60.\n5. Feb 15: Paid the dividend declared on January 31. Amount: 50.\n6. Feb 28: Recorded one month of interest on the bank note. Orontes has not paid it\n           yet. Amount: 4.\nRows 1 to 3 of Figure F01-06 are done for you. Complete rows 4 to 6. Then write the\njournal entries for rows 4 to 6.\n\nFIGURE F01-06. Faded effect grid for six Orontes Foods transactions in February; rows\n4 to 6 are for the learner.\n\nBOX — LANGUAGE FOCUS: Describing the effect of a transaction\n• debit X, credit Y: Debit Equipment, credit Cash.\n• X is debited (or credited) for an amount: Cash is debited for 1,500.\n• an increase in + the item; an increase of + the amount: an increase in assets of 2,380.\n• increase by (the change) and increase to (the new total): Equity increased by 1,530.\n• Exam stems often say: The effect of this transaction is to increase … and decrease …\n\nBOX — FALSE-FRIEND ALERT: Arabic and French traps in this section\n• استهلاك — Gulf texts use it for depreciation, but it also means consumption. Syrian\n  and Levant texts often say اهتلاك, and Egyptian texts say إهلاك. In English, always\n  say depreciation.\n• مخصص — Arabic uses one word for two different things. An allowance (for example,\n  the allowance for credit losses) reduces an asset. A provision is a liability. Never\n  say reserve for bad debts in a U.S. GAAP answer.\n• credit — Your bank credits your account when it receives your money, because the\n  bank now owes you. That is the bank's view. Do not use banking habits to guess the\n  accounting side.\n\nBOX — TERM BRIDGE: Key words in this section (15 rows)\ndouble-entry system | نظام القيد المزدوج\ndebit | مدين\ncredit | دائن\nnormal balance | الرصيد الطبيعي\njournal entry | قيد اليومية\nT-account | حساب على شكل حرف T\ntrial balance | ميزان المراجعة\ncontra account | حساب مقابل (حساب عكسي)\naccumulated depreciation | مجمع الإهلاك (مجمع الاستهلاك / الاهتلاك)\nallowance for credit losses | مخصص الخسائر الائتمانية\naccounts receivable | الذمم المدينة (المدينون)\naccounts payable | الذمم الدائنة (الدائنون)\ninventory | المخزون\ncost of goods sold | تكلفة البضاعة المباعة\nnotes payable | أوراق الدفع\n\nSECTION CHECK\nSC3-1  Which account normally has a credit balance?\n  A. Prepaid rent   B. Accumulated depreciation   C. Cost of goods sold   D. Dividends declared\nSC3-2  Orontes buys a bottling line for 1,200 in cash. Which journal entry is correct?\n  A. Debit Cash 1,200; credit Equipment 1,200\n  B. Debit Equipment expense 1,200; credit Cash 1,200\n  C. Debit Equipment 1,200; credit Notes payable 1,200\n  D. Debit Equipment 1,200; credit Cash 1,200\n\n================================================================================\n1.4  The accrual basis and the matching principle\n================================================================================\n\nWhen should a company record revenues and expenses? U.S. GAAP financial statements\nuse the accrual basis. Under the accrual basis, a company records revenue when it\nearns it, that is, when it delivers the goods or services. It records expenses when\nit incurs them. The timing of the cash does not decide the period. The cash basis\nrecords items only when cash is received or paid. The cash basis is simple, but it is\nnot acceptable under U.S. GAAP.\n\nFigure F01-07 shows why the difference matters. On December 20, 2025, Orontes ships\nolive oil to GreenBasket Supermarkets and sends an invoice for 90. The goods cost\nOrontes 54. GreenBasket pays on January 15, 2026.\n\nFIGURE F01-07. Timeline of a December sale collected in January: accrual basis versus\ncash basis.\n\nUnder the accrual basis, Orontes records the revenue and the cost of goods sold in\nfiscal 2025, the year it delivered the goods. The gross profit of 36 belongs to 2025.\nIn January, the cash receipt only changes one asset (the receivable) into another\nasset (cash). Under the cash basis, the revenue would fall in 2026, which is the\nwrong period.\n\nTHE MATCHING PRINCIPLE\nThe matching principle says: record expenses in the same period as the revenues they\nhelp to produce. There are three ways to apply it.\n• Cause and effect. Some costs link directly to a sale. Cost of goods sold is the\n  classic example: Orontes records it at the same time as the sales revenue.\n• Systematic and rational allocation. Some costs help many periods, such as a bottling\n  line. Depreciation spreads the cost over the years of use.\n• Immediate recognition. Some costs have no clear link to future revenue, such as most\n  advertising and office salaries. They are expenses in the period in which they are incurred.\n\nThe accrual basis creates four common types of adjustment. You met three of them in\nthe February transactions.\nPrepaid expense (deferral) | Cash is paid before the expense is incurred. |\n  Rent paid in advance (February, row 3) | an asset\nContract liability, or unearned revenue (deferral) | Cash is received before the\n  revenue is earned. | Hotel pays in advance (February, row 4) | a liability\nAccrued expense | The expense is incurred before cash is paid. | Interest owed on the\n  bank note (February, row 6) | a liability\nAccrued revenue | Revenue is earned before cash is received or billed. | Studied in\n  Chapter 11 | an asset\n\nTwo of these names are important for the exam. A prepaid expense is an asset: the\ncompany has paid for a future benefit. A contract liability (unearned revenue) is a\nliability: the company must still deliver the goods or services.\n\nBOX — EXAM TRAP: Cash is not the trigger\n• Cash received in advance is a liability, not revenue.\n• Cash paid in advance is an asset, not an expense.\n• Matching means matching expenses to revenues, not to cash payments.\n\nBOX — TERM BRIDGE: Key words in this section (5 rows)\naccrual basis | أساس الاستحقاق\ncash basis | الأساس النقدي\nmatching principle | مبدأ مقابلة الإيرادات بالمصروفات\nprepaid expense | مصروف مدفوع مقدماً\ncontract liability (unearned revenue) | التزام العقد (إيراد مقبوض مقدماً)\n\nSECTION CHECK\nSC4-1  On December 28, a customer pays Orontes 60 for goods that Orontes will deliver\n       in January. What does Orontes report at December 31?\n  A. A liability of 60   B. Revenue of 60   C. An asset of 60 and revenue of 60\n  D. Nothing until the goods are delivered\nSC4-2  Which cost is matched to revenue by cause and effect?\n  A. Depreciation of the Amman bottling line   B. Advertising for a new product\n  C. Cost of goods sold   D. Rent for the head office\n\n================================================================================\n1.5  Who writes the rules? U.S. GAAP and IFRS\n================================================================================\n\nIn the United States, the accounting rules are called U.S. GAAP (generally accepted\naccounting principles). The FASB (Financial Accounting Standards Board) writes them.\nThe FASB is a private, independent organization. It keeps all U.S. GAAP in one place:\nthe Accounting Standards Codification (ASC). The ASC is the single source of\nauthoritative nongovernmental U.S. GAAP. It is organized by topic numbers. For\nexample, ASC 606 covers revenue and ASC 842 covers leases. When the FASB changes a\nrule, it issues an Accounting Standards Update (ASU), which amends the Codification.\n\nThe SEC (Securities and Exchange Commission) is a U.S. government agency. It has\nlegal authority over the financial reporting of public companies, and it recognizes\nthe FASB as the standard setter. For companies that report to the SEC, SEC rules are\nalso part of authoritative GAAP. Do not confuse these bodies with the PCAOB, which\noversees the audits of public companies. The PCAOB sets auditing standards, not\naccounting standards.\n\nOutside the United States, many countries use IFRS (International Financial Reporting\nStandards). The IASB (International Accounting Standards Board) issues them, under\nthe IFRS Foundation. Many countries in the Middle East and North Africa, for example\nJordan and the United Arab Emirates, require IFRS for listed companies. In this book,\nCedar Retail S.A.L. is our IFRS company.\n\nFIGURE F01-08. Who writes the rules: SEC, FASB and the ASC for U.S. GAAP; IFRS\nFoundation and IASB for IFRS.\n\nThe CMA exam tests U.S. GAAP. However, Section A also asks about the main differences\nbetween U.S. GAAP and IFRS (Chapter 17). If you learned accounting under IFRS, this is\ngood news: most concepts are the same. Every chapter has an IFRS Contrast box, so\nChapter 17 will be revision for you.\n\nBoth systems rest on a conceptual framework: the basic ideas behind the standards,\nsuch as the users, the elements and the qualities of useful information. You studied\nthe U.S. version in Sections 1.1 and 1.2.\n\nBOX — IFRS CONTRAST: U.S. GAAP names and IFRS names\nbalance sheet | statement of financial position\nincome statement | statement of profit or loss\ncommon stock | share capital (ordinary shares)\nadditional paid-in capital | share premium\naccounts receivable | trade receivables\nnet income | profit\n\nBOX — EXAM TRAP: Four bodies, four jobs\n• FASB: writes U.S. GAAP (the ASC).\n• SEC: U.S. government agency with legal authority over public company reporting.\n• PCAOB: sets auditing standards for audits of public companies.\n• IASB: writes IFRS.\n\nBOX — TERM BRIDGE: Key words in this section (7 rows)\nU.S. GAAP | المبادئ المحاسبية المقبولة عموماً في الولايات المتحدة\nIFRS | المعايير الدولية لإعداد التقارير المالية\nFASB | مجلس معايير المحاسبة المالية\nAccounting Standards Codification (ASC) | تقنين معايير المحاسبة\nIASB | مجلس معايير المحاسبة الدولية\nSEC | هيئة الأوراق المالية والبورصات الأمريكية\nconceptual framework | الإطار المفاهيمي\n\nSECTION CHECK\nSC5-1  Which organization writes U.S. GAAP?\n  A. The FASB   B. The SEC   C. The PCAOB   D. The IASB\nSC5-2  The IFRS term share premium has the same meaning as the U.S. GAAP term:\n  A. retained earnings   B. common stock   C. net income   D. additional paid-in capital\n\n================================================================================\n1.6  A first look at the four statements\n================================================================================\n\nEach statement answers a different question for users. Chapters 2 to 5 study each\nstatement in detail. Here is the overview.\nBalance sheet | What does the company have and owe, and what is the owners' claim? |\n  at a date | Chapter 2\nIncome statement | How did the company perform? | for a period | Chapter 3\nStatement of changes in equity | Why did each equity account change? | for a period | Chapter 4\nStatement of cash flows | Where did cash come from, and where did it go? | for a period | Chapter 5\n\nThe four statements are linked. Figure F01-09 shows the two main links. Net income\nflows into retained earnings, and the net change in cash explains the cash balance on\nthe balance sheet.\n\nFIGURE F01-09. How the four statements connect through net income, retained earnings\nand cash.\n\nYou can see both links in the January transactions of Orontes. Net income was 80:\nrevenue of 300, minus cost of goods sold of 180, minus wages of 40. The dividend\ndeclared was 50, so retained earnings rose by 30. Cash rose by 1,060: an operating\noutflow of 40, an investing outflow of 1,200 and a financing inflow of 2,300.\n\nNotice that net income and the change in cash are very different numbers. This is the\naccrual basis at work.\n\nBefore you leave this chapter, practise reading a trial balance. A trial balance lists\nevery account balance and checks that total debits equal total credits. Figure F01-10\nis the trial balance of Barada Wholesale, Inc., a small food distributor that buys\nproducts from Orontes. You will use it in the section check below and in the practice set.\n\nFIGURE F01-10. Barada Wholesale, Inc. — trial balance, December 31, 2025 (USD 000)\nAccount | Debit | Credit\nCash | 1,050 |\nAccounts receivable | 300 |\nAllowance for credit losses | | 20\nInventory | 420 |\nPrepaid rent | 24 |\nLand | 936 |\nEquipment | 4,200 |\nAccumulated depreciation | | 900\nAccounts payable | | 380\nDividends payable | | 50\nNotes payable (long-term) | | 1,800\nCommon stock | | 600\nAdditional paid-in capital | | 1,900\nRetained earnings, January 1 | | 1,000\nDividends declared | 50 |\nSales revenue | | 3,100\nCost of goods sold | 1,860 |\nWages expense | 520 |\nDepreciation expense | 300 |\nInterest expense | 90 |\nTotals | 9,750 | 9,750\n\nBOX — TERM BRIDGE: Key words in this section (5 rows)\nbalance sheet | الميزانية العمومية\nstatement of financial position | قائمة المركز المالي\nincome statement | قائمة الدخل\nstatement of changes in equity | قائمة التغيرات في حقوق الملكية\nstatement of cash flows | قائمة التدفقات النقدية\n\nBOX — LANGUAGE FOCUS: Reading exam questions\n• MOST likely or BEST describes: more than one option may be partly true. Choose the best one.\n• NOT or EXCEPT: three options are true. Find the one that is false.\n• immediately after the transaction: ignore later events, such as the cash payment next month.\n• net effect: add the increases and decreases together before you answer.\n\nSECTION CHECK\nSC6-1  Which statement reports amounts at a single date?\n  A. The income statement   B. The balance sheet\n  C. The statement of changes in equity   D. The statement of cash flows\nSC6-2  Look at Figure F01-10. Which two accounts are contra-asset accounts?\n  A. Allowance for credit losses and dividends payable\n  B. Accumulated depreciation and notes payable\n  C. Allowance for credit losses and accumulated depreciation\n  D. Dividends declared and accumulated depreciation\n\n================================================================================\n1.7  Extended Example 1A: from a trial balance to a set of statements\n================================================================================\n\nThis example takes the Barada trial balance of Figure F01-10 and builds the statements\nfrom it, in the order a company prepares them. It is exam-core: the exam often gives a\ntrial balance and asks for one figure from one statement.\n\n1. THE PLAN\n• Sort every account by element: asset, liability, equity, revenue or expense.\n• Prepare the income statement first, because net income is needed by the next statement.\n• Prepare the statement of changes in equity, which produces closing retained earnings.\n• Prepare the balance sheet last, using that closing figure.\n• Check that the balance sheet balances.\n\n2. THE INCOME STATEMENT\nRevenue of $3,100 less cost of goods sold of $1,860 gives a gross profit of $1,240.\nOperating expenses are wages of $520 and depreciation of $300, so operating income is\n$420. Interest of $90 leaves net income of $330.\n\nFIGURE F01-11. Extended Example 1A: the income statement from the trial balance\nBarada Wholesale, Inc. — income statement, 2025 (USD 000) | Amount\nSales revenue | 3,100\nCost of goods sold | (1,860)\nGross profit | 1,240\nWages expense | (520)\nDepreciation expense | (300)\nOperating income | 420\nInterest expense | (90)\nNet income | 330\n\n3. THE STATEMENT OF CHANGES IN EQUITY\nRetained earnings open at $1,000, rise by net income of $330 and fall by dividends\ndeclared of $50, closing at $1,280. Common stock of $600 and additional paid-in capital\nof $1,900 do not change this year, so total equity is $3,780.\n\n4. THE BALANCE SHEET\nCurrent assets of $1,774 and non-current assets of $4,236 give total assets of $6,010.\nCurrent liabilities are $430 and the long-term note is $1,800.\n\nFIGURE F01-12. Extended Example 1A: the classified balance sheet\nBarada Wholesale, Inc. — balance sheet, December 31, 2025 (USD 000) | Amount\nCash | 1,050\nAccounts receivable, net of the allowance | 280\nInventory | 420\nPrepaid rent | 24\nTotal current assets | 1,774\nLand | 936\nEquipment, net of accumulated depreciation | 3,300\nTotal assets | 6,010\nAccounts payable and dividends payable | 430\nNotes payable (long-term) | 1,800\nTotal liabilities | 2,230\nCommon stock and additional paid-in capital | 2,500\nRetained earnings | 1,280\nTotal equity | 3,780\nTotal liabilities and equity | 6,010\n\nTwo contra accounts matter here. Receivables appear net of the allowance at $280, and\nequipment appears net of accumulated depreciation at $3,300. Reporting either one gross\nwould overstate assets.\n\n5. TIE LINE — BOX: WORKED EXAMPLE: Everything ties\n• Assets $6,010 = liabilities $2,230 + equity $3,780.\n• Operating income $420 − interest $90 = net income $330.\n• Opening retained earnings $1,000 + $330 − $50 = $1,280, the figure on the balance sheet.\n\n6. READING THE RESULT\nWorking capital is $1,344 and the current ratio is 4.13, so Barada can meet its\nshort-term obligations comfortably. A lender would also note that the long-term note of\n$1,800 is large against equity of $3,780.\n\n7. WHERE THE FOURTH STATEMENT COMES FROM\nA statement of cash flows needs last year's balance sheet as well as this one, so it\ncannot be built from a single trial balance. Chapter 5 prepares it for Orontes, which\nhas both years.\n\n8. HOW THE EXAM ASKS IT — BOX: EXAM TRAP: Exam view\nA trial balance question usually asks for one number: total current assets, closing\nretained earnings or net income. Sort the accounts by element first, and watch the\ncontra accounts, which belong with the asset they reduce rather than with liabilities.\n\n9. SELF-CHECK\nSC7-1  Use Extended Example 1A. What are Barada's total current assets?\n  A. 430   B. 1,774   C. 1,794   D. 6,010\nSC7-2  Use Extended Example 1A. What is Barada's closing retained earnings?\n  A. 330   B. 1,000   C. 1,280   D. 1,330\nSC7-3  Use Extended Example 1A. Where does the dividends payable balance appear?\n  A. In equity, with retained earnings   B. As a contra asset\n  C. In non-current liabilities   D. In current liabilities\n\n================================================================================\nCHAPTER SUMMARY\n================================================================================\nFIGURE F01-13. Chapter 1 summary map: the key rule for each of the seven topics.\n\nGeneral-purpose financial statements serve investors, lenders and other creditors.\nUseful information is relevant and faithfully represented. The statements are built\nfrom elements that the accounting equation links: assets equal liabilities plus equity.\nEvery transaction is recorded with equal debits and credits. The accrual basis records\nrevenues when they are earned and expenses when they are incurred, and it matches\nexpenses to the revenues they help to produce. The FASB writes U.S. GAAP in the ASC,\nand the IASB writes IFRS. The CMA exam uses U.S. GAAP and tests the main IFRS\ndifferences. The balance sheet shows one date, while the other three statements cover\na period, and net income and cash link them.\n\n================================================================================\nPRACTICE — MULTIPLE CHOICE P01–P16\n================================================================================\n(Full stems, options and per-option explanations are reproduced in the site data and\nin ch01/answers.html. Correct answers:)\nP01 A | P02 B | P03 A | P04 D | P05 C | P06 B | P07 B | P08 D\nP09 A | P10 D | P11 B | P12 D | P13 C | P14 A | P15 C | P16 D\n\n================================================================================\nCASE QUESTION — Barada Wholesale, Inc.\n================================================================================\nBarada Wholesale, Inc. is a food wholesaler in Amman and one of Orontes's largest\ncustomers. It buys tahini, halva and oils from Orontes and sells them to shops across\nJordan. Barada prepares its statements under U.S. GAAP because its bank and its main\nsupplier ask for them.\n\nAt the end of 2025 Barada's bookkeeper prepared a trial balance. It lists every account\nwith its debit or credit balance, and total debits equal total credits. The list\nincludes cash, receivables, inventory, prepaid rent, equipment and accumulated\ndepreciation, loans, dividends payable, revenue and several expenses.\n\nBarada's bank is reviewing its loan and wants three figures from the statements before\nit renews the credit line: net income for the year, total assets and total liabilities\nat the year-end. The bank also wants to know how the year's results changed retained\nearnings after closing.\n\nBarada's owner, Mr Haddad, asks you to help. He is not an accountant, so he wants each\naccount placed in the right category before any total is calculated. Amounts in the\ntrial balance are in thousands of U.S. dollars. He also wants to know whether dividends\nreduce net income, because a friend told him they do.\n\nHow to enter answers: Enter amounts in thousands of U.S. dollars (USD 000), without the\n$ sign or commas (for example, 1250).\n\nC1-1  Match each account to its category. (On the exam screen you would drag each\n      account into a box.)\n      Categories: Asset · Contra-asset · Liability · Equity · Revenue · Expense\n      Prepaid rent = Asset\n      Land = Asset\n      Dividends payable = Liability\n      Additional paid-in capital = Equity\n      Sales revenue = Revenue\n      Interest expense = Expense\n      Allowance for credit losses = Contra-asset\n      Accumulated depreciation = Contra-asset\n      Note: Watch the two contra-asset accounts: they sit with the assets but have\n      credit balances. Dividends payable is a liability; dividends declared (in the\n      trial balance) reduce equity.\nC1-2  Enter Barada's net income for 2025 (USD 000).  Answer: 330\n      280 is wrong: this answer subtracts the dividends declared as if they were an expense.\n      1,240 is wrong: this answer stops at gross profit (revenue minus cost of goods sold).\n      3,100 is wrong: this answer reports revenue instead of net income.\nC1-3  Enter Barada's total assets at December 31, 2025 (USD 000).  Answer: 6,010\n      6,030 is wrong: this answer ignores the allowance for credit losses.\n      6,910 is wrong: this answer ignores accumulated depreciation.\n      7,850 is wrong: this answer adds the contra-asset balances instead of subtracting them.\nC1-4  After closing, Barada's retained earnings at December 31, 2025 are: [select]\n      Options: 330 | 1,000 | 1,280 | 1,330       Answer: 1,280\n      1,330 is wrong: this answer forgets to subtract the dividends declared.\n      330 is wrong: this answer reports only this year's net income.\nC1-5  Enter Barada's total liabilities at December 31, 2025 (USD 000).  Answer: 2,230\n      2,280 is wrong: this answer also counts dividends declared (an equity item) as a liability.\n      2,180 is wrong: this answer leaves out dividends payable, which is a liability.\nC1-6  When Barada declares a cash dividend, the dividend reduces ____; it is not an expense.\n      Choices: retained earnings | net income | revenue | total assets on the declaration date\n      Answer: retained earnings\n      net income is wrong: this answer treats a dividend as an expense that reduces net income.\n      revenue is wrong: [sic — clause dropped in the source] a dividend is a distribution,\n        not a reduction of revenue.\n      total assets on the declaration date is wrong: [sic — clause dropped in the source]\n        cash leaves only when the dividend is paid; declaration creates a liability.\n\n================================================================================\nANSWERS AND EXPLANATIONS — as printed in the chapter\n(The site must use these rationales; they are the author's words.)\n================================================================================\n\nSECTION CHECKS\n\nSC1-1  Answer D. The primary users are existing and potential investors, lenders\nand other creditors.\nA is wrong. Managers can get internal information, so they are not primary users.\nB is wrong. Internal auditors work inside the company and use internal records.\nC is wrong. The board directs the company and can demand internal reports.\n\nSC1-2  Answer A. Predictive value is part of relevance: the information helps users\npredict the future.\nB is wrong. Verifiability means independent people could agree on the amount.\nC is wrong. Comparability means users can compare companies or periods.\nD is wrong. Timeliness means the information arrives in time to affect the decision.\n\nSC2-1  Answer B. Wages are a cost of the company's central operations, so they are\nan expense.\nA is wrong. A dividend is a distribution to owners. It reduces retained earnings but\nis not an expense.\nC is wrong. Repaying principal reduces a liability and an asset. No expense arises.\nD is wrong. A bottling line is an asset. Its cost becomes an expense later, through\ndepreciation.\n\nSC2-2  Answer A. Equity = assets - liabilities = 500 - 320 = 180.\nB is wrong. This is the liabilities figure, not equity.\nC is wrong. This is total assets, not equity.\nD is wrong. This adds liabilities to assets. Equity is the residual after deducting\nliabilities.\n\nSC3-1  Answer B. Accumulated depreciation is a contra-asset account, so it has the\nopposite (credit) balance to assets.\nA is wrong. Prepaid rent is an asset, so its normal balance is a debit.\nC is wrong. Cost of goods sold is an expense, so its normal balance is a debit.\nD is wrong. Dividends declared reduce equity, so they have a debit balance.\n\nSC3-2  Answer D. One asset increases (debit Equipment) and another asset decreases\n(credit Cash).\nA is wrong. The sides are reversed: this entry would increase cash and decrease\nequipment.\nB is wrong. The bottling line is an asset, not an expense of this period.\nC is wrong. Orontes paid cash; it did not borrow. The credit side must be Cash.\n\nSC4-1  Answer A. Cash increases, and Orontes owes the customer the goods. This is a\ncontract liability (unearned revenue).\nB is wrong. Revenue is not earned until the goods are delivered in January.\nC is wrong. The cash asset is correct, but the credit is a liability, not revenue.\nD is wrong. Cash has been received, so Orontes must record it now.\n\nSC4-2  Answer C. Cost of goods sold links directly to each sale, so it is recorded\nwith the sales revenue.\nA is wrong. Depreciation uses systematic and rational allocation over the years of use.\nB is wrong. Advertising has no clear link to specific revenue, so it is recognized\nimmediately.\nD is wrong. Head-office rent is a period cost, recognized immediately.\n\nSC5-1  Answer A. The FASB writes U.S. GAAP and keeps it in the Accounting Standards\nCodification.\nB is wrong. The SEC has legal authority, but it recognizes the FASB as the standard\nsetter.\nC is wrong. The PCAOB sets auditing standards, not accounting standards.\nD is wrong. The IASB writes IFRS, not U.S. GAAP.\n\nSC5-2  Answer D. Share premium is the amount received for shares above their par\nvalue: additional paid-in capital.\nA is wrong. Retained earnings come from past profits, not from selling shares.\nB is wrong. Common stock holds only the par value of the shares.\nC is wrong. Net income is a performance measure, not an equity account.\n\nSC6-1  Answer B. The balance sheet shows position at one date. The other statements\ncover a period.\nA is wrong. The income statement covers a period.\nC is wrong. The statement of changes in equity covers a period.\nD is wrong. The statement of cash flows covers a period.\n\nSC6-2  Answer C. Both accounts reduce an asset (receivables and equipment) and have\ncredit balances.\nA is wrong. Dividends payable is a liability, not a contra-asset.\nB is wrong. Notes payable is a liability, not a contra-asset.\nD is wrong. Dividends declared reduces equity, not an asset.\n\nSC7-1  Answer B. Cash, receivables net of the allowance, inventory and prepaid rent.\nA is wrong. This is total current liabilities.\nC is wrong. This adds the allowance back instead of subtracting it.\nD is wrong. This is total assets, including land and equipment.\n\nSC7-2  Answer C. Opening retained earnings plus net income less dividends declared.\nA is wrong. This is net income, not the retained earnings balance.\nB is wrong. This is the opening balance, before net income.\nD is wrong. This ignores the dividend declared.\n\nSC7-3  Answer D. It is an obligation to pay cash within a year.\nA is wrong. A declared dividend is a liability, not part of equity.\nB is wrong. It reduces nothing on the asset side.\nC is wrong. It is payable within the year.\n\nYOUR TURN: FEBRUARY, ROWS 4 TO 6\nRow 4. A Dubai hotel group paid cash in advance for pastries that Orontes will\ndeliver in March. Dr Cash 60; Cr Contract liability (unearned revenue) 60.\nAssets +60, liabilities +60, equity 0, net income 0; cash flow: operating +60.\nRow 5. Paid the dividend declared on January 31. Dr Dividends payable 50; Cr Cash 50.\nAssets (50), liabilities (50), equity 0, net income 0; cash flow: financing (50).\nRow 6. Recorded one month of interest on the bank note. Orontes has not paid it yet.\nDr Interest expense 4; Cr Interest payable 4. Assets 0, liabilities +4, equity (4),\nnet income (4); cash flow: none.\nRow 4 is a liability, not revenue: Orontes must still deliver the pastries. Row 5\nreduces a liability and cash; retained earnings fell in January, when the dividend\nwas declared. Row 6 is an accrued expense: interest is an expense of February even\nthough Orontes has not paid it.\n\nMULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS — full stems and options\n\nP01  Under the U.S. conceptual framework, the objective of general-purpose financial\nreporting is to provide useful information mainly to:\nA. existing and potential investors, lenders and other creditors.\nB. the board of directors and senior management.\nC. government statisticians and tax authorities.\nD. the company's employees and trade unions.\n\nP02  A bank is deciding whether to renew a five-year loan to Orontes. Which\ninformation will the bank find MOST useful?\nA. The highest and lowest share price during the year\nB. The company's ability to generate cash to pay interest and repay principal\nC. The number of products the company sells\nD. The names of the company's major shareholders\n\nP03  Two companies use the same accounting methods, so an analyst can compare their\nresults. This quality is called:\nA. comparability   B. relevance   C. verifiability   D. faithful representation\n\nP04  A company issues common stock for cash. What is the effect on the accounting\nequation?\nA. Assets increase and revenue increases.\nB. Assets increase and liabilities increase.\nC. There is no effect on the equation.\nD. Assets increase and equity increases.\n\nP05  The board declares a cash dividend that will be paid next month. What is the\neffect immediately after the declaration?\nA. Expenses increase and net income decreases.\nB. Assets decrease and equity decreases.\nC. Liabilities increase and equity decreases.\nD. There is no effect until the dividend is paid.\n\nP06  Which transaction changes total assets but does NOT change total equity?\nA. Paying wages in cash\nB. Borrowing cash from a bank\nC. Selling goods on credit at a profit\nD. Buying equipment for cash\n\nP07  A company has assets of 900 and liabilities of 350. It then borrows 100 in cash\nand declares and pays a cash dividend of 40. What is total equity after these\ntransactions?\nA. 450   B. 510   C. 550   D. 610\n\nP08  Which account normally has a debit balance?\nA. Accounts payable   B. Sales revenue   C. Additional paid-in capital   D. Prepaid rent\n\nP09  Accumulated depreciation is BEST described as:\nA. a contra-asset account with a credit balance.\nB. a liability for future asset replacement.\nC. an expense of the current period.\nD. a reduction of retained earnings.\n\nP10  Orontes ships goods on December 20, 2025 and invoices 90. The goods cost 54. The\ncustomer pays on January 15, 2026. The fiscal year ends on December 31. How much\nrevenue does Orontes report for fiscal 2025?\nA. 0   B. 36   C. 54   D. 90\n\nP11  Which statement about the matching principle is correct?\nA. Expenses are recognized in the period in which they are paid.\nB. Expenses are recognized in the same period as the revenues they help to produce.\nC. Revenues are recognized in the period in which cash is received.\nD. All costs are capitalized as assets until related revenue is earned.\n\nP12  On February 10, Orontes pays 24 for 12 months of warehouse rent in advance. How\ndoes the payment appear immediately after it is made?\nA. As an expense of 24   B. As a liability of 24\nC. It is not recorded until the end of the month.   D. As an asset of 24\n\nP13  Which statement explains why retained earnings changed during the year?\nA. The balance sheet   B. The statement of cash flows\nC. The statement of changes in equity   D. The income statement\n\nP14  Net income for the period flows directly into:\nA. retained earnings in the statement of changes in equity.\nB. the cash balance on the balance sheet.\nC. total liabilities.   D. common stock.\n\nP15  Which statement is TRUE?\nA. The PCAOB writes U.S. GAAP for public companies.\nB. The IASB writes U.S. GAAP for companies that also report under IFRS.\nC. The SEC has legal authority over public company reporting and recognizes the FASB\nas the standard setter.\nD. The ASC contains IFRS for U.S. companies.\n\nP16  A Jordanian company's IFRS statements show share premium of 400. Under U.S. GAAP,\nthe same item is called:\nA. retained earnings.   B. common stock.   C. treasury stock.\nD. additional paid-in capital.\n\nMULTIPLE-CHOICE QUESTIONS — ANSWERS\n\nP01  Answer A. These are the primary users. They provide resources and cannot demand\nspecial reports.\nB is wrong. Management can obtain internal information, so it is not a primary user.\nC is wrong. Governments use the statements, but they are not the primary users named\nby the framework.\nD is wrong. Employees are users, but not the primary users named by the framework.\n\nP02  Answer B. A lender's main question is repayment: can the borrower pay interest\nand principal on time?\nA is wrong. Share prices interest investors more than lenders and do not show\nrepayment ability.\nC is wrong. The product count does not show whether the company can repay the loan.\nD is wrong. Ownership details do not answer the lender's repayment question.\n\nP03  Answer A. Comparability lets users identify similarities and differences between\ncompanies or periods.\nB is wrong. Relevance means the information can make a difference to a decision.\nC is wrong. Verifiability means independent observers could reach the same result.\nD is wrong. Faithful representation means complete, neutral and free from error.\n\nP04  Answer D. Cash (an asset) increases, and contributed capital (equity) increases\nby the same amount.\nA is wrong. Selling shares is financing from owners, not revenue.\nB is wrong. Shares are equity, not debt. A liability arises only when the company\nborrows.\nC is wrong. Both sides of the equation increase, so the equation changes but still\nbalances.\n\nP05  Answer C. Declaring the dividend creates dividends payable (a liability) and\nreduces retained earnings.\nA is wrong. A dividend is a distribution to owners, not an expense.\nB is wrong. Cash decreases only on the payment date, next month.\nD is wrong. The obligation exists from the declaration date, so it is recorded now.\n\nP06  Answer B. Assets and liabilities both increase. Equity does not change.\nA is wrong. Wages are an expense, so equity (retained earnings) also decreases.\nC is wrong. A profitable sale increases equity through net income.\nD is wrong. One asset replaces another, so total assets do not change.\n\nP07  Answer B. Equity starts at 550 (900 - 350). Borrowing does not change equity.\nThe dividend reduces it by 40, so equity is 510.\nA is wrong. This is total liabilities after the loan (350 + 100), not equity.\nC is wrong. This ignores the dividend, which reduces retained earnings.\nD is wrong. This adds the loan to equity. A loan is a liability.\n\nP08  Answer D. Prepaid rent is an asset, and assets have debit balances.\nA is wrong. Accounts payable is a liability, with a credit balance.\nB is wrong. Revenue increases equity, so it has a credit balance.\nC is wrong. APIC is equity, with a credit balance.\n\nP09  Answer A. It reduces the cost of equipment on the balance sheet and has a credit\nbalance.\nB is wrong. The company owes nobody anything, so it is not a liability.\nC is wrong. Depreciation expense is the period's charge; accumulated depreciation is\nthe running total on the balance sheet.\nD is wrong. It reduces an asset, not equity directly.\n\nP10  Answer D. Under the accrual basis, revenue is recorded when the goods are\ndelivered, in 2025.\nA is wrong. This is cash-basis thinking. The cash timing does not decide the period.\nB is wrong. This is the gross profit (revenue minus cost), not the revenue.\nC is wrong. This is the cost of goods sold, not the revenue.\n\nP11  Answer B. This is the definition of matching.\nA is wrong. Payment timing does not decide when an expense is recognized.\nC is wrong. Revenue is recognized when earned, not when cash is received.\nD is wrong. Many costs (for example, advertising) are expensed immediately.\n\nP12  Answer D. Prepaid rent is an asset: Orontes has paid for 12 months of future use.\nA is wrong. The benefit is in the future, so the cost becomes an expense month by\nmonth.\nB is wrong. This confuses a prepaid expense (asset) with unearned revenue (liability).\nC is wrong. Cash has left the company, so the transaction must be recorded now.\n\nP13  Answer C. The statement of changes in equity shows beginning retained earnings,\nnet income, dividends and ending retained earnings.\nA is wrong. The balance sheet shows only the ending balance at one date.\nB is wrong. The statement of cash flows explains cash, not equity.\nD is wrong. The income statement shows net income but not dividends or the opening\nbalance.\n\nP14  Answer A. Net income is closed into retained earnings.\nB is wrong. Net income is not cash. The statement of cash flows explains the cash\nbalance.\nC is wrong. Net income belongs to the owners, so it goes to equity, not liabilities.\nD is wrong. Common stock changes only when shares are issued or retired.\n\nP15  Answer C. The SEC has the legal authority; the FASB sets the standards; the ASC\nholds U.S. GAAP.\nA is wrong. The PCAOB sets auditing standards.\nB is wrong. The IASB writes IFRS only.\nD is wrong. The ASC is U.S. GAAP. IFRS is a separate set of standards.\n\nP16  Answer D. Share premium and additional paid-in capital both mean the amount\nreceived above par value.\nA is wrong. Retained earnings come from profits, not from issuing shares.\nB is wrong. Common stock holds only the par value.\nC is wrong. Treasury stock is the company's own shares that it bought back (Chapter 4).\n\n================================================================================\nNOT IN THE CHAPTER — anything not above may not be taught by the site.\nIn particular the chapter contains NO Arabic for: comparability, verifiability,\ntimeliness, understandability, cost constraint, stewardship, predictive value,\nconfirmatory value, measure, record, present, comprehensive income, investments by\nowners, distributions to owners, contributed capital, ASU, PCAOB, accrued expense,\naccrued revenue, depreciation, provision, allowance, gross profit, operating income,\nworking capital, current ratio. Those 26 Arabic renderings were generated, not sourced.\n================================================================================\n\n================================================================================\nCHAPTER 1, EXTRACTED VERBATIM FROM CMA_P1_Book1_volume_v1.3.docx\nsha256 0836a0eb7cc8c7afedda8b6b364617f786477ff8b620f3fbae36ed6469e2557a\n================================================================================\nThe Language and Framework of Financial Reporting\nلغة التقارير المالية وإطارها\nFinancial reporting has its own language. You already know many of its ideas from your accounting studies in Arabic or French. In this chapter you meet the English words that the CMA exam uses for those ideas, and you see how U.S. rules organize them.\nWe start with the people who read financial statements. Then we look at the building blocks of the statements, the accounting equation, double entry and the accrual basis. We finish with the rule makers and a first look at the four statements. Figure F01-01 is your map.\nFigure F01-01. Roadmap of Chapter 1: seven linked topics from users to the four financial statements.\nLearning objectives\nLOS\nOfficial statement (ICMA, 2024)\nIn plain words\nDepth here\nA.1.a\nidentify the users of these financial statements and their needs\nIdentify the users of financial statements and what each user needs.\nFull treatment\nA.1.b\ndemonstrate an understanding of the purposes and uses of each statement\nState what each of the four financial statements is for.\nOverview (full in Chapters 2–5)\nA.1.e\nidentify how various financial transactions affect the elements of each of the financial statements and determine the proper classification of a given transaction\nShow how a transaction changes the accounting equation, and classify simple transactions.\nIntroduction (full in Chapters 2–5)\nA.2.z\ndemonstrate an understanding of the matching principle with respect to revenues and expenses, and be able to apply it to a specific situation\nExplain the accrual basis and the matching principle.\nIntroduction (full in Chapter 11)\nBackground\nBackground for this book (not an ICMA learning outcome).\nDescribe who sets U.S. GAAP and IFRS, and which rules the CMA exam uses.\nBackground\nThe official statements are quoted from the ICMA Learning Outcome Statements (effective September 1, 2024). The plain-words column restates them in simpler English. The exam may test any outcome up to Level C.\nKey terms in this chapter\nEnglish (exam term)\nالعربية\nFrançais\nfinancial statements\nالقوائم المالية\nétats financiers\nprimary users\nالمستخدمون الرئيسيون\nutilisateurs principaux\nasset\nأصل (أصول)\nactif\nliability\nالتزام (التزامات)\npassif / dette\nequity\nحقوق الملكية\ncapitaux propres\nrevenue\nإيراد (إيرادات)\nproduits / chiffre d'affaires\nexpense\nمصروف (مصروفات)\ncharge\nretained earnings\nالأرباح المحتجزة\nrésultats non distribués (report à nouveau)\ndividend\nتوزيعات الأرباح\ndividende\naccounting equation\nالمعادلة المحاسبية\néquation comptable\ndebit\nمدين\ndébit\ncredit\nدائن\ncrédit\naccrual basis\nأساس الاستحقاق\ncomptabilité d'engagement\nmatching principle\nمبدأ مقابلة الإيرادات بالمصروفات\nprincipe de rattachement des charges aux produits\nU.S. GAAP\nالمبادئ المحاسبية المقبولة عموماً في الولايات المتحدة\nPCGR américains (US GAAP)\nIFRS\nالمعايير الدولية لإعداد التقارير المالية\nnormes IFRS\nWHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know\nYou have studied accounting before, perhaps in Arabic or French. The ideas in this chapter are not new to you: assets and liabilities, debit and credit, the trial balance. Three things are new.\nThe English names. Some are close to your language and some are not. The False-Friend boxes warn you about words that look familiar but mean something else.\nThe U.S. rules. The CMA exam uses U.S. GAAP. If you learned IFRS, most ideas are the same, but some names and some rules differ. The IFRS Contrast boxes show where.\nThe exam wording. CMA questions use exact terms, and one word can change the answer. The Language Focus boxes help you read questions carefully.\n1.1  Who uses financial statements, and why?\nA company collects thousands of facts every day: sales, purchases, salaries and loans. Financial statements summarize these facts in a standard form for people outside the company. The four main statements are the balance sheet, the income statement, the statement of changes in equity and the statement of cash flows.\nMost companies prepare general-purpose financial statements. These are reports for many readers at the same time, not for one special reader. The U.S. conceptual framework names the most important readers. The primary users are existing and potential investors, lenders and other creditors. They give resources (money, goods or credit) to the company. They usually cannot ask the company for special reports, so they depend on the published statements.\nEach user makes a different decision, so each user looks for different information. Figure F01-02 summarizes the main users and their needs.\nFigure F01-02. Users of financial statements and what they need\nUser\nDecision\nKey question\nStatements used most\nInvestors (shareholders) — PRIMARY\nBuy, hold or sell shares\nWill the company create future cash flows and returns?\nIncome statement, cash flows, balance sheet\nLenders (banks, bondholders) — PRIMARY\nLend, renew or stop a loan\nCan the company pay interest and repay the principal on time?\nCash flows, balance sheet\nOther creditors (suppliers) — PRIMARY\nSell on credit, set credit terms\nWill the company pay its bills when due?\nBalance sheet (short-term items), cash flows\nEmployees and unions\nStay, negotiate pay\nIs the company stable and profitable?\nIncome statement\nCustomers\nSign long-term contracts\nWill the company continue to supply us?\nBalance sheet, income statement\nGovernments and regulators\nTax, oversight, statistics\nDoes the company follow the rules and pay its taxes?\nAll statements and notes\nManagers (internal)\nPlan and control operations\nNot a primary user: can use internal reports\nInternal management reports\nOther groups also read financial statements: employees, customers, governments, tax authorities and the public. Managers read them too. However, managers are not primary users of general-purpose statements, because they can get any internal information they need. Reporting for managers is management accounting, the subject of most of the CMA exam.\nWhat do all these users need? In simple terms, they ask three questions.\nWhat does the company have, and what does it owe? The balance sheet answers this.\nHow did the company perform in the period? The income statement answers this.\nWhere did cash come from, and where did it go? The statement of cash flows answers this.\nUsers also want to judge stewardship: how well the managers used the owners' resources.\nWhat makes information useful?\nThe conceptual framework lists the qualities of useful information. There are two fundamental qualities. Relevance means the information can make a difference to a decision. It helps users predict the future (predictive value) or check their past predictions (confirmatory value). Relevance includes materiality: an item is material if leaving it out or stating it wrongly could change users' decisions.\nFaithful representation means the information shows what it claims to show. It is complete, neutral and free from error. Four enhancing qualities make information even more useful: comparability, verifiability, timeliness and understandability. Finally, there is a cost constraint: the benefit of reporting information should be greater than its cost.\nEXAM TRAP   Who is a primary user?\nPrimary users are investors, lenders and other creditors. Managers are users, but not primary users of general-purpose statements. Also remember: relevance and faithful representation are the two fundamental qualities. Comparability, verifiability, timeliness and understandability are only enhancing qualities.\nLANGUAGE FOCUS   Five accounting verbs\nThese verbs have exact meanings in exam questions. Learn the difference.\n\nExample: Orontes recognizes revenue when it delivers the goods. It measures the revenue at the invoice price. It presents revenue in the income statement, and it discloses its revenue policy in the notes.\nTERM BRIDGE   Key words in this section\nSECTION CHECK   Test yourself\nSC1-1  Which group is a primary user of general-purpose financial statements?\nA.\tThe company's managers\nB.\tThe company's internal auditors\nC.\tThe company's board of directors\nD.\tExisting and potential lenders\nSC1-2  Information helps an investor forecast next year's cash flows. This information has:\nA.\tpredictive value\nB.\tverifiability\nC.\tcomparability\nD.\ttimeliness\nAnswers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.\n1.2  The building blocks: elements and the accounting equation\nEvery amount in the financial statements belongs to an element. The FASB conceptual framework defines ten elements. Three of them describe the balance sheet at one date.\nAn asset is a present right of an entity to an economic benefit. Examples: cash, accounts receivable, inventory and equipment.\nA liability is a present obligation of an entity to transfer an economic benefit. Examples: accounts payable, notes payable and wages payable.\nEquity is the residual interest in the assets after the liabilities are deducted. It is the owners' claim.\nThe other elements describe changes during a period.\nRevenues and expenses come from the company's central, ongoing operations. When Orontes sells olive oil, it earns revenue. When it pays plant workers, it has an expense.\nGains and losses come from peripheral or incidental events. If Orontes sells an old delivery truck for more than its carrying amount, it has a gain.\nInvestments by owners, such as buying new shares, increase equity. Distributions to owners, such as dividends, decrease equity.\nComprehensive income is the total change in equity from sources other than owners. You will meet it in Chapters 3 and 15.\nThe accounting equation connects the three balance sheet elements:\nAssets = Liabilities + Equity\nThe equation always holds, because creditors (liabilities) or owners (equity) finance every asset. Equity has two main parts. Contributed capital is what owners paid in: common stock at its par value, plus additional paid-in capital (APIC). Retained earnings (RE) are the past net income that the company kept instead of paying it out as dividends. Figure F01-03 shows the full picture.\nFigure F01-03. The accounting equation A = L + E, with equity split into contributed capital and retained earnings.\nLook at the lower part of Figure F01-03. Revenues and gains increase retained earnings. Expenses and losses decrease it. Dividends also decrease it, but a dividend is not an expense. It is a distribution to owners, so it never appears in the income statement.\n● IFRS CONTRAST   Same idea, different words\nThe IFRS Conceptual Framework (2018) defines an asset as a present economic resource controlled by the entity as a result of past events. The idea is the same as in U.S. GAAP; only the wording is different. IFRS also uses the word income for both revenue and gains, while U.S. GAAP keeps revenues and gains apart. You may also know these IFRS names: statement of financial position (balance sheet), share capital (common stock) and share premium (additional paid-in capital).\nFALSE-FRIEND ALERT   Words that look familiar\nstock — In U.S. English, stock means shares: common stock, a stock dividend. In French and British English, stock often means inventory. In a CMA question, inventory is always inventory.\nincome and revenue — Arabic دخل is not إيراد. Revenue is the gross amount from sales. Net income is what remains after expenses. Do not use income when the question means revenue.\nrésultat and charges — In French, résultat is the profit or loss (net income), and charges are expenses. In English, charges usually means fees.\nbalance — In French, la balance is the trial balance. The balance sheet is le bilan.\nTERM BRIDGE   Key words in this section\nSECTION CHECK   Test yourself\nSC2-1  Which item is an expense?\nA.\tA cash dividend paid to shareholders\nB.\tWages paid to plant workers\nC.\tRepayment of a bank loan\nD.\tPurchase of a new bottling line\nSC2-2  A company has total assets of 500 and total liabilities of 320. What is its equity?\nA.\t180\nB.\t320\nC.\t500\nD.\t820\nAnswers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.\n1.3  Double entry: debits and credits\nAccountants record every transaction with the double-entry system. Each transaction affects at least two accounts, and total debits always equal total credits. This is why the accounting equation always balances.\nDebit simply means the left side of an account, and credit means the right side. Neither word means good or bad, and neither word always means increase. Whether a debit increases an account depends on the type of account. Each account has a normal balance: the side that increases it. Figure F01-04 shows the rules.\nFigure F01-04. Debit and credit rules: which six account types increase with a debit and which with a credit.\nThe expanded equation at the bottom of Figure F01-04 is a useful memory aid. Accounts on the left side of the equation (assets, expenses and dividends) increase with a debit. Accounts on the right side (liabilities, equity and revenues) increase with a credit.\nA contra account reduces a related account, so it has the opposite normal balance. For example, accumulated depreciation reduces equipment. It has a credit balance, but it belongs with the assets. The allowance for credit losses reduces accounts receivable in the same way (Chapter 6).\nWORKED EXAMPLE   Orontes Foods in January\nOrontes Foods, Inc. makes olive oil and tahini in Amman, Jordan, and frozen pastries in Dubai. Its shares are listed on a U.S. stock exchange, and it reports under U.S. GAAP. Here are its journal entries for six transactions in January 2025. Amounts are in USD 000.\n\nCheck each entry: the debits equal the credits. Figure F01-05 shows the effect of each entry on the four statements.\nFigure F01-05. Effect grid for six Orontes Foods transactions in January 2025 (USD 000).\nRead the grid row by row. Three points matter for the exam.\nRows 1 and 2 bring in cash of 2,300 in total, but no revenue. Money from owners and lenders is financing. It is not income.\nRow 4 creates revenue of 300 but no cash. Orontes records revenue when it delivers the goods, not when the customer pays.\nRow 6 reduces retained earnings by 50 and creates a liability. No cash moves until February, and net income does not change.\nThe totals prove the equation. Assets rose by 2,380, liabilities rose by 850 and equity rose by 1,530.\nEXAM TRAP   Three classic mistakes\nA dividend is not an expense. It reduces retained earnings, not net income.\nCash from borrowing or from issuing shares is not revenue. It is financing.\nBuying equipment for cash is not an expense today. One asset (cash) becomes another asset (equipment). The cost becomes an expense later, through depreciation.\nYOUR TURN   Orontes Foods in February\nHere are six transactions from February (USD 000).\n1. Feb 3: Paid a supplier for olives bought on credit in January. Amount: 150.\n2. Feb 8: Collected part of the January receivable from GreenBasket Supermarkets. Amount: 200.\n3. Feb 10: Paid 12 months of warehouse rent in advance. Amount: 24.\n4. Feb 12: A Dubai hotel group paid cash in advance for pastries that Orontes will deliver in March. Amount: 60.\n5. Feb 15: Paid the dividend declared on January 31. Amount: 50.\n6. Feb 28: Recorded one month of interest on the bank note. Orontes has not paid it yet. Amount: 4.\nRows 1 to 3 of Figure F01-06 are done for you. Complete rows 4 to 6. Then write the journal entries for rows 4 to 6. The answers are at the end of the chapter.\nFigure F01-06. Faded effect grid for six Orontes Foods transactions in February; rows 4 to 6 are for the learner.\nLANGUAGE FOCUS   Describing the effect of a transaction\ndebit X, credit Y: Debit Equipment, credit Cash.\nX is debited (or credited) for an amount: Cash is debited for 1,500.\nan increase in + the item; an increase of + the amount: an increase in assets of 2,380.\nincrease by (the change) and increase to (the new total): Equity increased by 1,530.\nExam stems often say: The effect of this transaction is to increase … and decrease …\nFALSE-FRIEND ALERT   Arabic and French traps in this section\nاستهلاك — Gulf texts use it for depreciation, but it also means consumption. Syrian and Levant texts often say اهتلاك, and Egyptian texts say إهلاك. In English, always say depreciation.\nمخصص — Arabic uses one word for two different things. An allowance (for example, the allowance for credit losses) reduces an asset. A provision is a liability. Never say reserve for bad debts in a U.S. GAAP answer.\ncredit — Your bank credits your account when it receives your money, because the bank now owes you. That is the bank's view. Do not use banking habits to guess the accounting side.\nTERM BRIDGE   Key words in this section\nSECTION CHECK   Test yourself\nSC3-1  Which account normally has a credit balance?\nA.\tPrepaid rent\nB.\tAccumulated depreciation\nC.\tCost of goods sold\nD.\tDividends declared\nSC3-2  Orontes buys a bottling line for 1,200 in cash. Which journal entry is correct?\nA.\tDebit Cash 1,200; credit Equipment 1,200\nB.\tDebit Equipment expense 1,200; credit Cash 1,200\nC.\tDebit Equipment 1,200; credit Notes payable 1,200\nD.\tDebit Equipment 1,200; credit Cash 1,200\nAnswers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.\n1.4  The accrual basis and the matching principle\nWhen should a company record revenues and expenses? U.S. GAAP financial statements use the accrual basis. Under the accrual basis, a company records revenue when it earns it, that is, when it delivers the goods or services. It records expenses when it incurs them. The timing of the cash does not decide the period. The cash basis records items only when cash is received or paid. The cash basis is simple, but it is not acceptable under U.S. GAAP.\nFigure F01-07 shows why the difference matters. On December 20, 2025, Orontes ships olive oil to GreenBasket Supermarkets and sends an invoice for 90. The goods cost Orontes 54. GreenBasket pays on January 15, 2026.\nFigure F01-07. Timeline of a December sale collected in January: accrual basis versus cash basis.\nUnder the accrual basis, Orontes records the revenue and the cost of goods sold in fiscal 2025, the year it delivered the goods. The gross profit of 36 belongs to 2025. In January, the cash receipt only changes one asset (the receivable) into another asset (cash). Under the cash basis, the revenue would fall in 2026, which is the wrong period.\nThe matching principle\nThe matching principle says: record expenses in the same period as the revenues they help to produce. There are three ways to apply it.\nCause and effect. Some costs link directly to a sale. Cost of goods sold is the classic example: Orontes records it at the same time as the sales revenue.\nSystematic and rational allocation. Some costs help many periods, such as a bottling line. Depreciation spreads the cost over the years of use.\nImmediate recognition. Some costs have no clear link to future revenue, such as most advertising and office salaries. They are expenses in the period in which they are incurred.\nThe accrual basis creates four common types of adjustment. You met three of them in the February transactions.\nType\nWhat happens\nOrontes example\nCreates\nPrepaid expense (deferral)\nCash is paid before the expense is incurred.\nRent paid in advance (February, row 3)\nan asset\nContract liability, or unearned revenue (deferral)\nCash is received before the revenue is earned.\nHotel pays in advance (February, row 4)\na liability\nAccrued expense\nThe expense is incurred before cash is paid.\nInterest owed on the bank note (February, row 6)\na liability\nAccrued revenue\nRevenue is earned before cash is received or billed.\nStudied in Chapter 11\nan asset\nTwo of these names are important for the exam. A prepaid expense is an asset: the company has paid for a future benefit. A contract liability (unearned revenue) is a liability: the company must still deliver the goods or services.\nEXAM TRAP   Cash is not the trigger\nCash received in advance is a liability, not revenue.\nCash paid in advance is an asset, not an expense.\nMatching means matching expenses to revenues, not to cash payments.\nTERM BRIDGE   Key words in this section\nSECTION CHECK   Test yourself\nSC4-1  On December 28, a customer pays Orontes 60 for goods that Orontes will deliver in January. What does Orontes report at December 31?\nA.\tA liability of 60\nB.\tRevenue of 60\nC.\tAn asset of 60 and revenue of 60\nD.\tNothing until the goods are delivered\nSC4-2  Which cost is matched to revenue by cause and effect?\nA.\tDepreciation of the Amman bottling line\nB.\tAdvertising for a new product\nC.\tCost of goods sold\nD.\tRent for the head office\nAnswers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.\n1.5  Who writes the rules? U.S. GAAP and IFRS\nIn the United States, the accounting rules are called U.S. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles). The FASB (Financial Accounting Standards Board) writes them. The FASB is a private, independent organization. It keeps all U.S. GAAP in one place: the Accounting Standards Codification (ASC). The ASC is the single source of authoritative nongovernmental U.S. GAAP. It is organized by topic numbers. For example, ASC 606 covers revenue and ASC 842 covers leases. When the FASB changes a rule, it issues an Accounting Standards Update (ASU), which amends the Codification.\nThe SEC (Securities and Exchange Commission) is a U.S. government agency. It has legal authority over the financial reporting of public companies, and it recognizes the FASB as the standard setter. For companies that report to the SEC, SEC rules are also part of authoritative GAAP. Do not confuse these bodies with the PCAOB, which oversees the audits of public companies. The PCAOB sets auditing standards, not accounting standards.\nOutside the United States, many countries use IFRS (International Financial Reporting Standards). The IASB (International Accounting Standards Board) issues them, under the IFRS Foundation. Many countries in the Middle East and North Africa, for example Jordan and the United Arab Emirates, require IFRS for listed companies. In this book, Cedar Retail S.A.L. is our IFRS company. Figure F01-08 shows both systems.\nFigure F01-08. Who writes the rules: SEC, FASB and the ASC for U.S. GAAP; IFRS Foundation and IASB for IFRS.\nThe CMA exam tests U.S. GAAP. However, Section A also asks about the main differences between U.S. GAAP and IFRS (Chapter 17). If you learned accounting under IFRS, this is good news: most concepts are the same. Every chapter has an IFRS Contrast box, so Chapter 17 will be revision for you.\nBoth systems rest on a conceptual framework: the basic ideas behind the standards, such as the users, the elements and the qualities of useful information. You studied the U.S. version in Sections 1.1 and 1.2.\n● IFRS CONTRAST   U.S. GAAP names and IFRS names\nEXAM TRAP   Four bodies, four jobs\nFASB: writes U.S. GAAP (the ASC).\nSEC: U.S. government agency with legal authority over public company reporting.\nPCAOB: sets auditing standards for audits of public companies.\nIASB: writes IFRS.\nTERM BRIDGE   Key words in this section\nSECTION CHECK   Test yourself\nSC5-1  Which organization writes U.S. GAAP?\nA.\tThe FASB\nB.\tThe SEC\nC.\tThe PCAOB\nD.\tThe IASB\nSC5-2  The IFRS term share premium has the same meaning as the U.S. GAAP term:\nA.\tretained earnings\nB.\tcommon stock\nC.\tnet income\nD.\tadditional paid-in capital\nAnswers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.\n1.6  A first look at the four statements\nEach statement answers a different question for users. Chapters 2 to 5 study each statement in detail. Here is the overview.\nStatement\nQuestion it answers\nTime\nChapter\nBalance sheet\nWhat does the company have and owe, and what is the owners' claim?\nat a date\n2\nIncome statement\nHow did the company perform?\nfor a period\n3\nStatement of changes in equity\nWhy did each equity account change?\nfor a period\n4\nStatement of cash flows\nWhere did cash come from, and where did it go?\nfor a period\n5\nThe four statements are linked. Figure F01-09 shows the two main links. Net income flows into retained earnings, and the net change in cash explains the cash balance on the balance sheet.\nFigure F01-09. How the four statements connect through net income, retained earnings and cash.\nYou can see both links in the January transactions of Orontes. Net income was 80: revenue of 300, minus cost of goods sold of 180, minus wages of 40. The dividend declared was 50, so retained earnings rose by 30. Cash rose by 1,060: an operating outflow of 40, an investing outflow of 1,200 and a financing inflow of 2,300.\nNotice that net income and the change in cash are very different numbers. This is the accrual basis at work.\nBefore you leave this chapter, practise reading a trial balance. A trial balance lists every account balance and checks that total debits equal total credits. Figure F01-10 is the trial balance of Barada Wholesale, Inc., a small food distributor that buys products from Orontes. You will use it in the section check below and in the practice set.\nFigure F01-10. Barada Wholesale, Inc. — trial balance, December 31, 2025 (USD 000)\nAccount\nDebit\nCredit\nCash\n1,050\n\nAccounts receivable\n300\n\nAllowance for credit losses\n\n20\nInventory\n420\n\nPrepaid rent\n24\n\nLand\n936\n\nEquipment\n4,200\n\nAccumulated depreciation\n\n900\nAccounts payable\n\n380\nDividends payable\n\n50\nNotes payable (long-term)\n\n1,800\nCommon stock\n\n600\nAdditional paid-in capital\n\n1,900\nRetained earnings, January 1\n\n1,000\nDividends declared\n50\n\nSales revenue\n\n3,100\nCost of goods sold\n1,860\n\nWages expense\n520\n\nDepreciation expense\n300\n\nInterest expense\n90\n\nTotals\n9,750\n9,750\nTERM BRIDGE   Key words in this section\nLANGUAGE FOCUS   Reading exam questions\nMOST likely or BEST describes: more than one option may be partly true. Choose the best one.\nNOT or EXCEPT: three options are true. Find the one that is false.\nimmediately after the transaction: ignore later events, such as the cash payment next month.\nnet effect: add the increases and decreases together before you answer.\nSECTION CHECK   Test yourself\nSC6-1  Which statement reports amounts at a single date?\nA.\tThe income statement\nB.\tThe balance sheet\nC.\tThe statement of changes in equity\nD.\tThe statement of cash flows\nSC6-2  Look at Figure F01-10. Which two accounts are contra-asset accounts?\nA.\tAllowance for credit losses and dividends payable\nB.\tAccumulated depreciation and notes payable\nC.\tAllowance for credit losses and accumulated depreciation\nD.\tDividends declared and accumulated depreciation\nAnswers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.\n1.7  Extended Example 1A: from a trial balance to a set of statements\nThis example takes the Barada trial balance of Figure F01-10 and builds the statements from it, in the order a company prepares them. It is exam-core: the exam often gives a trial balance and asks for one figure from one statement.\n1. The plan\nSort every account by element: asset, liability, equity, revenue or expense.\nPrepare the income statement first, because net income is needed by the next statement.\nPrepare the statement of changes in equity, which produces closing retained earnings.\nPrepare the balance sheet last, using that closing figure.\nCheck that the balance sheet balances.\n2. The income statement\nRevenue of $3,100 less cost of goods sold of $1,860 gives a gross profit of $1,240. Operating expenses are wages of $520 and depreciation of $300, so operating income is $420. Interest of $90 leaves net income of $330. Figure F01-11 sets it out.\nFigure F01-11. Extended Example 1A: the income statement from the trial balance\nBarada Wholesale, Inc. — income statement, 2025 (USD 000)\nAmount\nSales revenue\n3,100\nCost of goods sold\n(1,860)\nGross profit\n1,240\nWages expense\n(520)\nDepreciation expense\n(300)\nOperating income\n420\nInterest expense\n(90)\nNet income\n330\n3. The statement of changes in equity\nRetained earnings open at $1,000, rise by net income of $330 and fall by dividends declared of $50, closing at $1,280. Common stock of $600 and additional paid-in capital of $1,900 do not change this year, so total equity is $3,780.\n4. The balance sheet\nCurrent assets of $1,774 and non-current assets of $4,236 give total assets of $6,010. Current liabilities are $430 and the long-term note is $1,800. Figure F01-12 sets out the classified balance sheet.\nFigure F01-12. Extended Example 1A: the classified balance sheet\nBarada Wholesale, Inc. — balance sheet, December 31, 2025 (USD 000)\nAmount\nCash\n1,050\nAccounts receivable, net of the allowance\n280\nInventory\n420\nPrepaid rent\n24\nTotal current assets\n1,774\nLand\n936\nEquipment, net of accumulated depreciation\n3,300\nTotal assets\n6,010\nAccounts payable and dividends payable\n430\nNotes payable (long-term)\n1,800\nTotal liabilities\n2,230\nCommon stock and additional paid-in capital\n2,500\nRetained earnings\n1,280\nTotal equity\n3,780\nTotal liabilities and equity\n6,010\nTwo contra accounts matter here. Receivables appear net of the allowance at $280, and equipment appears net of accumulated depreciation at $3,300. Reporting either one gross would overstate assets.\n5. Tie line\nWORKED EXAMPLE   Everything ties\nAssets $6,010 = liabilities $2,230 + equity $3,780.\nOperating income $420 − interest $90 = net income $330.\nOpening retained earnings $1,000 + $330 − $50 = $1,280, the figure on the balance sheet.\n6. Reading the result\nWorking capital is $1,344 and the current ratio is 4.13, so Barada can meet its short-term obligations comfortably. A lender would also note that the long-term note of $1,800 is large against equity of $3,780.\n7. Where the fourth statement comes from\nA statement of cash flows needs last year's balance sheet as well as this one, so it cannot be built from a single trial balance. Chapter 5 prepares it for Orontes, which has both years.\n8. How the exam asks it\nEXAM TRAP   Exam view\nA trial balance question usually asks for one number: total current assets, closing retained earnings or net income. Sort the accounts by element first, and watch the contra accounts, which belong with the asset they reduce rather than with liabilities.\n9. Self-check\nSECTION CHECK   Test yourself\nSC7-1  Use Extended Example 1A. What are Barada's total current assets?\nA.\t430\nB.\t1,774\nC.\t1,794\nD.\t6,010\nSC7-2  Use Extended Example 1A. What is Barada's closing retained earnings?\nA.\t330\nB.\t1,000\nC.\t1,280\nD.\t1,330\nSC7-3  Use Extended Example 1A. Where does the dividends payable balance appear?\nA.\tIn equity, with retained earnings\nB.\tAs a contra asset\nC.\tIn non-current liabilities\nD.\tIn current liabilities\nAnswers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.\nChapter summary\nFigure F01-13 brings the chapter together. Each box now shows the key rule for its topic.\nFigure F01-13. Chapter 1 summary map: the key rule for each of the seven topics.\nGeneral-purpose financial statements serve investors, lenders and other creditors. Useful information is relevant and faithfully represented. The statements are built from elements that the accounting equation links: assets equal liabilities plus equity. Every transaction is recorded with equal debits and credits. The accrual basis records revenues when they are earned and expenses when they are incurred, and it matches expenses to the revenues they help to produce. The FASB writes U.S. GAAP in the ASC, and the IASB writes IFRS. The CMA exam uses U.S. GAAP and tests the main IFRS differences. The balance sheet shows one date, while the other three statements cover a period, and net income and cash link them.\nPractice\nAnswer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case question: like the exam, it gives a short case and an exhibit, followed by independent items (drag-and-drop, select from a list, fill in the blank and calculations). The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.\nMultiple-choice questions\nP01  Under the U.S. conceptual framework, the objective of general-purpose financial reporting is to provide useful information mainly to:\nA.\texisting and potential investors, lenders and other creditors.\nB.\tthe board of directors and senior management.\nC.\tgovernment statisticians and tax authorities.\nD.\tthe company's employees and trade unions.\nP02  A bank is deciding whether to renew a five-year loan to Orontes. Which information will the bank find MOST useful?\nA.\tThe highest and lowest share price during the year\nB.\tThe company's ability to generate cash to pay interest and repay principal\nC.\tThe number of products the company sells\nD.\tThe names of the company's major shareholders\nP03  Two companies use the same accounting methods, so an analyst can compare their results. This quality is called:\nA.\tcomparability\nB.\trelevance\nC.\tverifiability\nD.\tfaithful representation\nP04  A company issues common stock for cash. What is the effect on the accounting equation?\nA.\tAssets increase and revenue increases.\nB.\tAssets increase and liabilities increase.\nC.\tThere is no effect on the equation.\nD.\tAssets increase and equity increases.\nP05  The board declares a cash dividend that will be paid next month. What is the effect immediately after the declaration?\nA.\tExpenses increase and net income decreases.\nB.\tAssets decrease and equity decreases.\nC.\tLiabilities increase and equity decreases.\nD.\tThere is no effect until the dividend is paid.\nP06  Which transaction changes total assets but does NOT change total equity?\nA.\tPaying wages in cash\nB.\tBorrowing cash from a bank\nC.\tSelling goods on credit at a profit\nD.\tBuying equipment for cash\nP07  A company has assets of 900 and liabilities of 350. It then borrows 100 in cash and declares and pays a cash dividend of 40. What is total equity after these transactions?\nA.\t450\nB.\t510\nC.\t550\nD.\t610\nP08  Which account normally has a debit balance?\nA.\tAccounts payable\nB.\tSales revenue\nC.\tAdditional paid-in capital\nD.\tPrepaid rent\nP09  Accumulated depreciation is BEST described as:\nA.\ta contra-asset account with a credit balance.\nB.\ta liability for future asset replacement.\nC.\tan expense of the current period.\nD.\ta reduction of retained earnings.\nP10  Orontes ships goods on December 20, 2025 and invoices 90. The goods cost 54. The customer pays on January 15, 2026. The fiscal year ends on December 31. How much revenue does Orontes report for fiscal 2025?\nA.\t0\nB.\t36\nC.\t54\nD.\t90\nP11  Which statement about the matching principle is correct?\nA.\tExpenses are recognized in the period in which they are paid.\nB.\tExpenses are recognized in the same period as the revenues they help to produce.\nC.\tRevenues are recognized in the period in which cash is received.\nD.\tAll costs are capitalized as assets until related revenue is earned.\nP12  On February 10, Orontes pays 24 for 12 months of warehouse rent in advance. How does the payment appear immediately after it is made?\nA.\tAs an expense of 24\nB.\tAs a liability of 24\nC.\tIt is not recorded until the end of the month.\nD.\tAs an asset of 24\nP13  Which statement explains why retained earnings changed during the year?\nA.\tThe balance sheet\nB.\tThe statement of cash flows\nC.\tThe statement of changes in equity\nD.\tThe income statement\nP14  Net income for the period flows directly into:\nA.\tretained earnings in the statement of changes in equity.\nB.\tthe cash balance on the balance sheet.\nC.\ttotal liabilities.\nD.\tcommon stock.\nP15  Which statement is TRUE?\nA.\tThe PCAOB writes U.S. GAAP for public companies.\nB.\tThe IASB writes U.S. GAAP for companies that also report under IFRS.\nC.\tThe SEC has legal authority over public company reporting and recognizes the FASB as the standard setter.\nD.\tThe ASC contains IFRS for U.S. companies.\nP16  A Jordanian company's IFRS statements show share premium of 400. Under U.S. GAAP, the same item is called:\nA.\tretained earnings.\nB.\tcommon stock.\nC.\ttreasury stock.\nD.\tadditional paid-in capital.\nCase question: Barada Wholesale, Inc.\nBarada Wholesale, Inc. is a food wholesaler in Amman and one of Orontes's largest customers. It buys tahini, halva and oils from Orontes and sells them to shops across Jordan. Barada prepares its statements under U.S. GAAP because its bank and its main supplier ask for them.\nAt the end of 2025 Barada's bookkeeper prepared a trial balance. It lists every account with its debit or credit balance, and total debits equal total credits. The list includes cash, receivables, inventory, prepaid rent, equipment and accumulated depreciation, loans, dividends payable, revenue and several expenses.\nBarada's bank is reviewing its loan and wants three figures from the statements before it renews the credit line: net income for the year, total assets and total liabilities at the year-end. The bank also wants to know how the year's results changed retained earnings after closing.\nBarada's owner, Mr Haddad, asks you to help. He is not an accountant, so he wants each account placed in the right category before any total is calculated. Amounts in the trial balance are in thousands of U.S. dollars. He also wants to know whether dividends reduce net income, because a friend told him they do.\nUse the trial balance in Figure F01-10 for items C1-1 to C1-6.\nHow to enter answers: Enter amounts in thousands of U.S. dollars (USD 000), without the $ sign or commas (for example, 1250).\nC1-1  Match each account to its category. (On the exam screen you would drag each account into a box.)\nCategories: Asset · Contra-asset · Liability · Equity · Revenue · Expense\nAccount\nCategory\nPrepaid rent\n\nLand\n\nDividends payable\n\nAdditional paid-in capital\n\nSales revenue\n\nInterest expense\n\nAllowance for credit losses\n\nAccumulated depreciation\n\nC1-2  Enter Barada's net income for 2025 (USD 000).\nAnswer: ____________\nC1-3  Enter Barada's total assets at December 31, 2025 (USD 000).\nAnswer: ____________\nC1-4  After closing, Barada's retained earnings at December 31, 2025 are: [select]\nOptions: 330   |   1,000   |   1,280   |   1,330\nC1-5  Enter Barada's total liabilities at December 31, 2025 (USD 000).\nAnswer: ____________\nC1-6  When Barada declares a cash dividend, the dividend reduces ____; it is not an expense.\nChoose the word or phrase for the blank: retained earnings   |   net income   |   revenue   |   total assets on the declaration date\nAnswers and explanations\nSection checks\nSC1-1  Answer D. The primary users are existing and potential investors, lenders and other creditors.\nA is wrong. Managers can get internal information, so they are not primary users.\nB is wrong. Internal auditors work inside the company and use internal records.\nC is wrong. The board directs the company and can demand internal reports.\nSC1-2  Answer A. Predictive value is part of relevance: the information helps users predict the future.\nB is wrong. Verifiability means independent people could agree on the amount.\nC is wrong. Comparability means users can compare companies or periods.\nD is wrong. Timeliness means the information arrives in time to affect the decision.\nSC2-1  Answer B. Wages are a cost of the company's central operations, so they are an expense.\nA is wrong. A dividend is a distribution to owners. It reduces retained earnings but is not an expense.\nC is wrong. Repaying principal reduces a liability and an asset. No expense arises.\nD is wrong. A bottling line is an asset. Its cost becomes an expense later, through depreciation.\nSC2-2  Answer A. Equity = assets − liabilities = 500 − 320 = 180.\nB is wrong. This is the liabilities figure, not equity.\nC is wrong. This is total assets, not equity.\nD is wrong. This adds liabilities to assets. Equity is the residual after deducting liabilities.\nSC3-1  Answer B. Accumulated depreciation is a contra-asset account, so it has the opposite (credit) balance to assets.\nA is wrong. Prepaid rent is an asset, so its normal balance is a debit.\nC is wrong. Cost of goods sold is an expense, so its normal balance is a debit.\nD is wrong. Dividends declared reduce equity, so they have a debit balance.\nSC3-2  Answer D. One asset increases (debit Equipment) and another asset decreases (credit Cash).\nA is wrong. The sides are reversed: this entry would increase cash and decrease equipment.\nB is wrong. The bottling line is an asset, not an expense of this period.\nC is wrong. Orontes paid cash; it did not borrow. The credit side must be Cash.\nSC4-1  Answer A. Cash increases, and Orontes owes the customer the goods. This is a contract liability (unearned revenue).\nB is wrong. Revenue is not earned until the goods are delivered in January.\nC is wrong. The cash asset is correct, but the credit is a liability, not revenue.\nD is wrong. Cash has been received, so Orontes must record it now.\nSC4-2  Answer C. Cost of goods sold links directly to each sale, so it is recorded with the sales revenue.\nA is wrong. Depreciation uses systematic and rational allocation over the years of use.\nB is wrong. Advertising has no clear link to specific revenue, so it is recognized immediately.\nD is wrong. Head-office rent is a period cost, recognized immediately.\nSC5-1  Answer A. The FASB writes U.S. GAAP and keeps it in the Accounting Standards Codification.\nB is wrong. The SEC has legal authority, but it recognizes the FASB as the standard setter.\nC is wrong. The PCAOB sets auditing standards, not accounting standards.\nD is wrong. The IASB writes IFRS, not U.S. GAAP.\nSC5-2  Answer D. Share premium is the amount received for shares above their par value: additional paid-in capital.\nA is wrong. Retained earnings come from past profits, not from selling shares.\nB is wrong. Common stock holds only the par value of the shares.\nC is wrong. Net income is a performance measure, not an equity account.\nSC6-1  Answer B. The balance sheet shows position at one date. The other statements cover a period.\nA is wrong. The income statement covers a period.\nC is wrong. The statement of changes in equity covers a period.\nD is wrong. The statement of cash flows covers a period.\nSC6-2  Answer C. Both accounts reduce an asset (receivables and equipment) and have credit balances.\nA is wrong. Dividends payable is a liability, not a contra-asset.\nB is wrong. Notes payable is a liability, not a contra-asset.\nD is wrong. Dividends declared reduces equity, not an asset.\nSC7-1  Answer B. Cash, receivables net of the allowance, inventory and prepaid rent.\nA is wrong. This is total current liabilities.\nC is wrong. This adds the allowance back instead of subtracting it.\nD is wrong. This is total assets, including land and equipment.\nSC7-2  Answer C. Opening retained earnings plus net income less dividends declared.\nA is wrong. This is net income, not the retained earnings balance.\nB is wrong. This is the opening balance, before net income.\nD is wrong. This ignores the dividend declared.\nSC7-3  Answer D. It is an obligation to pay cash within a year.\nA is wrong. A declared dividend is a liability, not part of equity.\nB is wrong. It reduces nothing on the asset side.\nC is wrong. It is payable within the year.\nYour turn: February, rows 4 to 6\nRow\nTransaction\nJournal entry\nEffects\n4\nA Dubai hotel group paid cash in advance for pastries that Orontes will deliver in March.\nDr Cash 60; Cr Contract liability (unearned revenue) 60\nAssets +60, liabilities +60, equity 0, net income 0; cash flow: operating +60.\n5\nPaid the dividend declared on January 31.\nDr Dividends payable 50; Cr Cash 50\nAssets (50), liabilities (50), equity 0, net income 0; cash flow: financing (50).\n6\nRecorded one month of interest on the bank note. Orontes has not paid it yet.\nDr Interest expense 4; Cr Interest payable 4\nAssets 0, liabilities +4, equity (4), net income (4); cash flow: none.\nRow 4 is a liability, not revenue: Orontes must still deliver the pastries. Row 5 reduces a liability and cash; retained earnings fell in January, when the dividend was declared. Row 6 is an accrued expense: interest is an expense of February even though Orontes has not paid it.\nMultiple-choice questions\nP01  Answer A. These are the primary users. They provide resources and cannot demand special reports.\nB is wrong. Management can obtain internal information, so it is not a primary user.\nC is wrong. Governments use the statements, but they are not the primary users named by the framework.\nD is wrong. Employees are users, but not the primary users named by the framework.\nP02  Answer B. A lender's main question is repayment: can the borrower pay interest and principal on time?\nA is wrong. Share prices interest investors more than lenders and do not show repayment ability.\nC is wrong. The product count does not show whether the company can repay the loan.\nD is wrong. Ownership details do not answer the lender's repayment question.\nP03  Answer A. Comparability lets users identify similarities and differences between companies or periods.\nB is wrong. Relevance means the information can make a difference to a decision.\nC is wrong. Verifiability means independent observers could reach the same result.\nD is wrong. Faithful representation means complete, neutral and free from error.\nP04  Answer D. Cash (an asset) increases, and contributed capital (equity) increases by the same amount.\nA is wrong. Selling shares is financing from owners, not revenue.\nB is wrong. Shares are equity, not debt. A liability arises only when the company borrows.\nC is wrong. Both sides of the equation increase, so the equation changes but still balances.\nP05  Answer C. Declaring the dividend creates dividends payable (a liability) and reduces retained earnings.\nA is wrong. A dividend is a distribution to owners, not an expense.\nB is wrong. Cash decreases only on the payment date, next month.\nD is wrong. The obligation exists from the declaration date, so it is recorded now.\nP06  Answer B. Assets and liabilities both increase. Equity does not change.\nA is wrong. Wages are an expense, so equity (retained earnings) also decreases.\nC is wrong. A profitable sale increases equity through net income.\nD is wrong. One asset replaces another, so total assets do not change.\nP07  Answer B. Equity starts at 550 (900 − 350). Borrowing does not change equity. The dividend reduces it by 40, so equity is 510.\nA is wrong. This is total liabilities after the loan (350 + 100), not equity.\nC is wrong. This ignores the dividend, which reduces retained earnings.\nD is wrong. This adds the loan to equity. A loan is a liability.\nP08  Answer D. Prepaid rent is an asset, and assets have debit balances.\nA is wrong. Accounts payable is a liability, with a credit balance.\nB is wrong. Revenue increases equity, so it has a credit balance.\nC is wrong. APIC is equity, with a credit balance.\nP09  Answer A. It reduces the cost of equipment on the balance sheet and has a credit balance.\nB is wrong. The company owes nobody anything, so it is not a liability.\nC is wrong. Depreciation expense is the period's charge; accumulated depreciation is the running total on the balance sheet.\nD is wrong. It reduces an asset, not equity directly.\nP10  Answer D. Under the accrual basis, revenue is recorded when the goods are delivered, in 2025.\nA is wrong. This is cash-basis thinking. The cash timing does not decide the period.\nB is wrong. This is the gross profit (revenue minus cost), not the revenue.\nC is wrong. This is the cost of goods sold, not the revenue.\nP11  Answer B. This is the definition of matching.\nA is wrong. Payment timing does not decide when an expense is recognized.\nC is wrong. Revenue is recognized when earned, not when cash is received.\nD is wrong. Many costs (for example, advertising) are expensed immediately.\nP12  Answer D. Prepaid rent is an asset: Orontes has paid for 12 months of future use.\nA is wrong. The benefit is in the future, so the cost becomes an expense month by month.\nB is wrong. This confuses a prepaid expense (asset) with unearned revenue (liability).\nC is wrong. Cash has left the company, so the transaction must be recorded now.\nP13  Answer C. The statement of changes in equity shows beginning retained earnings, net income, dividends and ending retained earnings.\nA is wrong. The balance sheet shows only the ending balance at one date.\nB is wrong. The statement of cash flows explains cash, not equity.\nD is wrong. The income statement shows net income but not dividends or the opening balance.\nP14  Answer A. Net income is closed into retained earnings.\nB is wrong. Net income is not cash. The statement of cash flows explains the cash balance.\nC is wrong. Net income belongs to the owners, so it goes to equity, not liabilities.\nD is wrong. Common stock changes only when shares are issued or retired.\nP15  Answer C. The SEC has the legal authority; the FASB sets the standards; the ASC holds U.S. GAAP.\nA is wrong. The PCAOB sets auditing standards.\nB is wrong. The IASB writes IFRS only.\nD is wrong. The ASC is U.S. GAAP. IFRS is a separate set of standards.\nP16  Answer D. Share premium and additional paid-in capital both mean the amount received above par value.\nA is wrong. Retained earnings come from profits, not from issuing shares.\nB is wrong. Common stock holds only the par value.\nC is wrong. Treasury stock is the company's own shares that it bought back (Chapter 4).\nCase question: Barada Wholesale, Inc.\nC1-1\nAccount\nCategory\nPrepaid rent\nAsset\nLand\nAsset\nDividends payable\nLiability\nAdditional paid-in capital\nEquity\nSales revenue\nRevenue\nInterest expense\nExpense\nAllowance for credit losses\nContra-asset\nAccumulated depreciation\nContra-asset\nWatch the two contra-asset accounts: they sit with the assets but have credit balances. Dividends payable is a liability; dividends declared (in the trial balance) reduce equity.\nC1-2  Answer: 330.\n280 is wrong: this answer subtracts the dividends declared as if they were an expense.\n1,240 is wrong: this answer stops at gross profit (revenue minus cost of goods sold).\n3,100 is wrong: this answer reports revenue instead of net income.\nC1-3  Answer: 6,010.\n6,030 is wrong: this answer ignores the allowance for credit losses.\n6,910 is wrong: this answer ignores accumulated depreciation.\n7,850 is wrong: this answer adds the contra-asset balances instead of subtracting them.\nC1-4  Answer: 1,280.\n1,330 is wrong: this answer forgets to subtract the dividends declared.\n330 is wrong: this answer reports only this year's net income.\nC1-5  Answer: 2,230.\n2,280 is wrong: this answer also counts dividends declared (an equity item) as a liability.\n2,180 is wrong: this answer leaves out dividends payable, which is a liability.\nC1-6  Answer: retained earnings.\nnet income is wrong: this answer treats a dividend as an expense that reduces net income.\nrevenue is wrong: this answer a dividend is a distribution, not a reduction of revenue.\ntotal assets on the declaration date is wrong: this answer cash leaves only when the dividend is paid; declaration creates a liability.\nPART I — FOUNDATIONS AND THE FOUR FINANCIAL STATEMENTS\nChapter 2\n\n================================================================================\nCHAPTER 1, EXTRACTED VERBATIM FROM CMA_P1_Book1_volume_v1.3.docx\nsha256 0836a0eb7cc8c7afedda8b6b364617f786477ff8b620f3fbae36ed6469e2557a\n================================================================================\nThe Language and Framework of Financial Reporting\nلغة التقارير المالية وإطارها\nFinancial reporting has its own language. You already know many of its ideas from your accounting studies in Arabic or French. In this chapter you meet the English words that the CMA exam uses for those ideas, and you see how U.S. rules organize them.\nWe start with the people who read financial statements. Then we look at the building blocks of the statements, the accounting equation, double entry and the accrual basis. We finish with the rule makers and a first look at the four statements. Figure F01-01 is your map.\nFigure F01-01. Roadmap of Chapter 1: seven linked topics from users to the four financial statements.\nLearning objectives\nLOS\nOfficial statement (ICMA, 2024)\nIn plain words\nDepth here\nA.1.a\nidentify the users of these financial statements and their needs\nIdentify the users of financial statements and what each user needs.\nFull treatment\nA.1.b\ndemonstrate an understanding of the purposes and uses of each statement\nState what each of the four financial statements is for.\nOverview (full in Chapters 2–5)\nA.1.e\nidentify how various financial transactions affect the elements of each of the financial statements and determine the proper classification of a given transaction\nShow how a transaction changes the accounting equation, and classify simple transactions.\nIntroduction (full in Chapters 2–5)\nA.2.z\ndemonstrate an understanding of the matching principle with respect to revenues and expenses, and be able to apply it to a specific situation\nExplain the accrual basis and the matching principle.\nIntroduction (full in Chapter 11)\nBackground\nBackground for this book (not an ICMA learning outcome).\nDescribe who sets U.S. GAAP and IFRS, and which rules the CMA exam uses.\nBackground\nThe official statements are quoted from the ICMA Learning Outcome Statements (effective September 1, 2024). The plain-words column restates them in simpler English. The exam may test any outcome up to Level C.\nKey terms in this chapter\nEnglish (exam term)\nالعربية\nFrançais\nfinancial statements\nالقوائم المالية\nétats financiers\nprimary users\nالمستخدمون الرئيسيون\nutilisateurs principaux\nasset\nأصل (أصول)\nactif\nliability\nالتزام (التزامات)\npassif / dette\nequity\nحقوق الملكية\ncapitaux propres\nrevenue\nإيراد (إيرادات)\nproduits / chiffre d'affaires\nexpense\nمصروف (مصروفات)\ncharge\nretained earnings\nالأرباح المحتجزة\nrésultats non distribués (report à nouveau)\ndividend\nتوزيعات الأرباح\ndividende\naccounting equation\nالمعادلة المحاسبية\néquation comptable\ndebit\nمدين\ndébit\ncredit\nدائن\ncrédit\naccrual basis\nأساس الاستحقاق\ncomptabilité d'engagement\nmatching principle\nمبدأ مقابلة الإيرادات بالمصروفات\nprincipe de rattachement des charges aux produits\nU.S. GAAP\nالمبادئ المحاسبية المقبولة عموماً في الولايات المتحدة\nPCGR américains (US GAAP)\nIFRS\nالمعايير الدولية لإعداد التقارير المالية\nnormes IFRS\nWHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know\nYou have studied accounting before, perhaps in Arabic or French. The ideas in this chapter are not new to you: assets and liabilities, debit and credit, the trial balance. Three things are new.\nThe English names. Some are close to your language and some are not. The False-Friend boxes warn you about words that look familiar but mean something else.\nThe U.S. rules. The CMA exam uses U.S. GAAP. If you learned IFRS, most ideas are the same, but some names and some rules differ. The IFRS Contrast boxes show where.\nThe exam wording. CMA questions use exact terms, and one word can change the answer. The Language Focus boxes help you read questions carefully.\n1.1  Who uses financial statements, and why?\nA company collects thousands of facts every day: sales, purchases, salaries and loans. Financial statements summarize these facts in a standard form for people outside the company. The four main statements are the balance sheet, the income statement, the statement of changes in equity and the statement of cash flows.\nMost companies prepare general-purpose financial statements. These are reports for many readers at the same time, not for one special reader. The U.S. conceptual framework names the most important readers. The primary users are existing and potential investors, lenders and other creditors. They give resources (money, goods or credit) to the company. They usually cannot ask the company for special reports, so they depend on the published statements.\nEach user makes a different decision, so each user looks for different information. Figure F01-02 summarizes the main users and their needs.\nFigure F01-02. Users of financial statements and what they need\nUser\nDecision\nKey question\nStatements used most\nInvestors (shareholders) — PRIMARY\nBuy, hold or sell shares\nWill the company create future cash flows and returns?\nIncome statement, cash flows, balance sheet\nLenders (banks, bondholders) — PRIMARY\nLend, renew or stop a loan\nCan the company pay interest and repay the principal on time?\nCash flows, balance sheet\nOther creditors (suppliers) — PRIMARY\nSell on credit, set credit terms\nWill the company pay its bills when due?\nBalance sheet (short-term items), cash flows\nEmployees and unions\nStay, negotiate pay\nIs the company stable and profitable?\nIncome statement\nCustomers\nSign long-term contracts\nWill the company continue to supply us?\nBalance sheet, income statement\nGovernments and regulators\nTax, oversight, statistics\nDoes the company follow the rules and pay its taxes?\nAll statements and notes\nManagers (internal)\nPlan and control operations\nNot a primary user: can use internal reports\nInternal management reports\nOther groups also read financial statements: employees, customers, governments, tax authorities and the public. Managers read them too. However, managers are not primary users of general-purpose statements, because they can get any internal information they need. Reporting for managers is management accounting, the subject of most of the CMA exam.\nWhat do all these users need? In simple terms, they ask three questions.\nWhat does the company have, and what does it owe? The balance sheet answers this.\nHow did the company perform in the period? The income statement answers this.\nWhere did cash come from, and where did it go? The statement of cash flows answers this.\nUsers also want to judge stewardship: how well the managers used the owners' resources.\nWhat makes information useful?\nThe conceptual framework lists the qualities of useful information. There are two fundamental qualities. Relevance means the information can make a difference to a decision. It helps users predict the future (predictive value) or check their past predictions (confirmatory value). Relevance includes materiality: an item is material if leaving it out or stating it wrongly could change users' decisions.\nFaithful representation means the information shows what it claims to show. It is complete, neutral and free from error. Four enhancing qualities make information even more useful: comparability, verifiability, timeliness and understandability. Finally, there is a cost constraint: the benefit of reporting information should be greater than its cost.\nEXAM TRAP   Who is a primary user?\nPrimary users are investors, lenders and other creditors. Managers are users, but not primary users of general-purpose statements. Also remember: relevance and faithful representation are the two fundamental qualities. Comparability, verifiability, timeliness and understandability are only enhancing qualities.\nLANGUAGE FOCUS   Five accounting verbs\nThese verbs have exact meanings in exam questions. Learn the difference.\n\nExample: Orontes recognizes revenue when it delivers the goods. It measures the revenue at the invoice price. It presents revenue in the income statement, and it discloses its revenue policy in the notes.\nVerb\nMeaning in financial reporting\nrecognize\ninclude an item in the statements, with a name and an amount\nmeasure\ndecide the amount of the item\nrecord\nenter the item in the accounts (journal and ledger)\npresent\nshow the item on the face of a statement\ndisclose\nexplain the item in the notes to the statements\nTERM BRIDGE   Key words in this section\nEnglish (exam term)\nالعربية\nFrançais\nfinancial statements\nالقوائم المالية\nétats financiers\ngeneral-purpose financial statements\nالقوائم المالية ذات الغرض العام\nétats financiers à usage général\nprimary users\nالمستخدمون الرئيسيون\nutilisateurs principaux\nrelevance\nالملاءمة\npertinence\nfaithful representation\nالتمثيل الصادق\nreprésentation fidèle\nmateriality\nالأهمية النسبية\nimportance relative (matérialité)\nrecognize\nيعترف (الاعتراف)\ncomptabiliser (reconnaître)\ndisclose\nيُفصح (الإفصاح)\nprésenter en annexe (divulguer)\nSECTION CHECK   Test yourself\nSC1-1  Which group is a primary user of general-purpose financial statements?\nA.\tThe company's managers\nB.\tThe company's internal auditors\nC.\tThe company's board of directors\nD.\tExisting and potential lenders\nSC1-2  Information helps an investor forecast next year's cash flows. This information has:\nA.\tpredictive value\nB.\tverifiability\nC.\tcomparability\nD.\ttimeliness\nAnswers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.\n1.2  The building blocks: elements and the accounting equation\nEvery amount in the financial statements belongs to an element. The FASB conceptual framework defines ten elements. Three of them describe the balance sheet at one date.\nAn asset is a present right of an entity to an economic benefit. Examples: cash, accounts receivable, inventory and equipment.\nA liability is a present obligation of an entity to transfer an economic benefit. Examples: accounts payable, notes payable and wages payable.\nEquity is the residual interest in the assets after the liabilities are deducted. It is the owners' claim.\nThe other elements describe changes during a period.\nRevenues and expenses come from the company's central, ongoing operations. When Orontes sells olive oil, it earns revenue. When it pays plant workers, it has an expense.\nGains and losses come from peripheral or incidental events. If Orontes sells an old delivery truck for more than its carrying amount, it has a gain.\nInvestments by owners, such as buying new shares, increase equity. Distributions to owners, such as dividends, decrease equity.\nComprehensive income is the total change in equity from sources other than owners. You will meet it in Chapters 3 and 15.\nThe accounting equation connects the three balance sheet elements:\nAssets = Liabilities + Equity\nThe equation always holds, because creditors (liabilities) or owners (equity) finance every asset. Equity has two main parts. Contributed capital is what owners paid in: common stock at its par value, plus additional paid-in capital (APIC). Retained earnings (RE) are the past net income that the company kept instead of paying it out as dividends. Figure F01-03 shows the full picture.\nFigure F01-03. The accounting equation A = L + E, with equity split into contributed capital and retained earnings.\nLook at the lower part of Figure F01-03. Revenues and gains increase retained earnings. Expenses and losses decrease it. Dividends also decrease it, but a dividend is not an expense. It is a distribution to owners, so it never appears in the income statement.\n● IFRS CONTRAST   Same idea, different words\nThe IFRS Conceptual Framework (2018) defines an asset as a present economic resource controlled by the entity as a result of past events. The idea is the same as in U.S. GAAP; only the wording is different. IFRS also uses the word income for both revenue and gains, while U.S. GAAP keeps revenues and gains apart. You may also know these IFRS names: statement of financial position (balance sheet), share capital (common stock) and share premium (additional paid-in capital).\nFALSE-FRIEND ALERT   Words that look familiar\nstock — In U.S. English, stock means shares: common stock, a stock dividend. In French and British English, stock often means inventory. In a CMA question, inventory is always inventory.\nincome and revenue — Arabic دخل is not إيراد. Revenue is the gross amount from sales. Net income is what remains after expenses. Do not use income when the question means revenue.\nrésultat and charges — In French, résultat is the profit or loss (net income), and charges are expenses. In English, charges usually means fees.\nbalance — In French, la balance is the trial balance. The balance sheet is le bilan.\nTERM BRIDGE   Key words in this section\nEnglish (exam term)\nالعربية\nFrançais\nasset\nأصل (أصول)\nactif\nliability\nالتزام (التزامات)\npassif / dette\nequity\nحقوق الملكية\ncapitaux propres\nrevenue\nإيراد (إيرادات)\nproduits / chiffre d'affaires\nexpense\nمصروف (مصروفات)\ncharge\ngain\nمكسب (مكاسب)\nprofit / gain\nloss\nخسارة\nperte\nnet income\nصافي الدخل\nrésultat net\nretained earnings\nالأرباح المحتجزة\nrésultats non distribués (report à nouveau)\ncommon stock\nالأسهم العادية\nactions ordinaires / capital social\nadditional paid-in capital\nعلاوة إصدار الأسهم (رأس المال الإضافي المدفوع)\nprime d'émission\npar value\nالقيمة الاسمية\nvaleur nominale\ndividend\nتوزيعات الأرباح\ndividende\naccounting equation\nالمعادلة المحاسبية\néquation comptable\nSECTION CHECK   Test yourself\nSC2-1  Which item is an expense?\nA.\tA cash dividend paid to shareholders\nB.\tWages paid to plant workers\nC.\tRepayment of a bank loan\nD.\tPurchase of a new bottling line\nSC2-2  A company has total assets of 500 and total liabilities of 320. What is its equity?\nA.\t180\nB.\t320\nC.\t500\nD.\t820\nAnswers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.\n1.3  Double entry: debits and credits\nAccountants record every transaction with the double-entry system. Each transaction affects at least two accounts, and total debits always equal total credits. This is why the accounting equation always balances.\nDebit simply means the left side of an account, and credit means the right side. Neither word means good or bad, and neither word always means increase. Whether a debit increases an account depends on the type of account. Each account has a normal balance: the side that increases it. Figure F01-04 shows the rules.\nFigure F01-04. Debit and credit rules: which six account types increase with a debit and which with a credit.\nThe expanded equation at the bottom of Figure F01-04 is a useful memory aid. Accounts on the left side of the equation (assets, expenses and dividends) increase with a debit. Accounts on the right side (liabilities, equity and revenues) increase with a credit.\nA contra account reduces a related account, so it has the opposite normal balance. For example, accumulated depreciation reduces equipment. It has a credit balance, but it belongs with the assets. The allowance for credit losses reduces accounts receivable in the same way (Chapter 6).\nWORKED EXAMPLE   Orontes Foods in January\nOrontes Foods, Inc. makes olive oil and tahini in Amman, Jordan, and frozen pastries in Dubai. Its shares are listed on a U.S. stock exchange, and it reports under U.S. GAAP. Here are its journal entries for six transactions in January 2025. Amounts are in USD 000.\n\nCheck each entry: the debits equal the credits. Figure F01-05 shows the effect of each entry on the four statements.\n#\nDate\nAccount and explanation\nDebit\nCredit\n1\nJan 2\nCash\n1,500\n\n\n\nCommon stock\n\n100\n\n\nAdditional paid-in capital\n\n1,400\n\n\nIssued 100,000 new shares of $1 par common stock for $15 per share, in cash.\n\n\n2\nJan 5\nCash\n800\n\n\n\nNotes payable (long-term)\n\n800\n\n\nBorrowed cash from Levant Commerce Bank on a 5-year note at 6% interest.\n\n\n3\nJan 10\nEquipment\n1,200\n\n\n\nCash\n\n1,200\n\n\nBought a new bottling line for the Amman plant and paid cash.\n\n\n4\nJan 18\nAccounts receivable\n300\n\n\n\nSales revenue\n\n300\n\n\nCost of goods sold\n180\n\n\n\nInventory\n\n180\n\n\nSold olive oil to GreenBasket Supermarkets on credit. The goods had cost Orontes less than the selling price.\n\n\n5\nJan 31\nWages expense\n40\n\n\n\nCash\n\n40\n\n\nPaid January wages to plant workers in cash.\n\n\n6\nJan 31\nRetained earnings (dividends declared)\n50\n\n\n\nDividends payable\n\n50\n\n\nThe board declared a cash dividend, payable on February 15.\n\n\n\n\nTotals\n4,070\n4,070\nFigure F01-05. Effect grid for six Orontes Foods transactions in January 2025 (USD 000).\nRead the grid row by row. Three points matter for the exam.\nRows 1 and 2 bring in cash of 2,300 in total, but no revenue. Money from owners and lenders is financing. It is not income.\nRow 4 creates revenue of 300 but no cash. Orontes records revenue when it delivers the goods, not when the customer pays.\nRow 6 reduces retained earnings by 50 and creates a liability. No cash moves until February, and net income does not change.\nThe totals prove the equation. Assets rose by 2,380, liabilities rose by 850 and equity rose by 1,530.\nEXAM TRAP   Three classic mistakes\nA dividend is not an expense. It reduces retained earnings, not net income.\nCash from borrowing or from issuing shares is not revenue. It is financing.\nBuying equipment for cash is not an expense today. One asset (cash) becomes another asset (equipment). The cost becomes an expense later, through depreciation.\nYOUR TURN   Orontes Foods in February\nHere are six transactions from February (USD 000).\n1. Feb 3: Paid a supplier for olives bought on credit in January. Amount: 150.\n2. Feb 8: Collected part of the January receivable from GreenBasket Supermarkets. Amount: 200.\n3. Feb 10: Paid 12 months of warehouse rent in advance. Amount: 24.\n4. Feb 12: A Dubai hotel group paid cash in advance for pastries that Orontes will deliver in March. Amount: 60.\n5. Feb 15: Paid the dividend declared on January 31. Amount: 50.\n6. Feb 28: Recorded one month of interest on the bank note. Orontes has not paid it yet. Amount: 4.\nRows 1 to 3 of Figure F01-06 are done for you. Complete rows 4 to 6. Then write the journal entries for rows 4 to 6. The answers are at the end of the chapter.\nFigure F01-06. Faded effect grid for six Orontes Foods transactions in February; rows 4 to 6 are for the learner.\nLANGUAGE FOCUS   Describing the effect of a transaction\ndebit X, credit Y: Debit Equipment, credit Cash.\nX is debited (or credited) for an amount: Cash is debited for 1,500.\nan increase in + the item; an increase of + the amount: an increase in assets of 2,380.\nincrease by (the change) and increase to (the new total): Equity increased by 1,530.\nExam stems often say: The effect of this transaction is to increase … and decrease …\nFALSE-FRIEND ALERT   Arabic and French traps in this section\nاستهلاك — Gulf texts use it for depreciation, but it also means consumption. Syrian and Levant texts often say اهتلاك, and Egyptian texts say إهلاك. In English, always say depreciation.\nمخصص — Arabic uses one word for two different things. An allowance (for example, the allowance for credit losses) reduces an asset. A provision is a liability. Never say reserve for bad debts in a U.S. GAAP answer.\ncredit — Your bank credits your account when it receives your money, because the bank now owes you. That is the bank's view. Do not use banking habits to guess the accounting side.\nTERM BRIDGE   Key words in this section\nEnglish (exam term)\nالعربية\nFrançais\ndouble-entry system\nنظام القيد المزدوج\ncomptabilité en partie double\ndebit\nمدين\ndébit\ncredit\nدائن\ncrédit\nnormal balance\nالرصيد الطبيعي\nsolde normal\njournal entry\nقيد اليومية\nécriture de journal\nT-account\nحساب على شكل حرف T\ncompte en T\ntrial balance\nميزان المراجعة\nbalance (de vérification)\ncontra account\nحساب مقابل (حساب عكسي)\ncompte de contrepartie / correctif\naccumulated depreciation\nمجمع الإهلاك (مجمع الاستهلاك / الاهتلاك)\namortissements cumulés\nallowance for credit losses\nمخصص الخسائر الائتمانية\ndépréciation des créances\naccounts receivable\nالذمم المدينة (المدينون)\ncréances clients\naccounts payable\nالذمم الدائنة (الدائنون)\ndettes fournisseurs\ninventory\nالمخزون\nstocks\ncost of goods sold\nتكلفة البضاعة المباعة\ncoût des ventes (coût des marchandises vendues)\nnotes payable\nأوراق الدفع\neffets à payer / emprunts\nSECTION CHECK   Test yourself\nSC3-1  Which account normally has a credit balance?\nA.\tPrepaid rent\nB.\tAccumulated depreciation\nC.\tCost of goods sold\nD.\tDividends declared\nSC3-2  Orontes buys a bottling line for 1,200 in cash. Which journal entry is correct?\nA.\tDebit Cash 1,200; credit Equipment 1,200\nB.\tDebit Equipment expense 1,200; credit Cash 1,200\nC.\tDebit Equipment 1,200; credit Notes payable 1,200\nD.\tDebit Equipment 1,200; credit Cash 1,200\nAnswers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.\n1.4  The accrual basis and the matching principle\nWhen should a company record revenues and expenses? U.S. GAAP financial statements use the accrual basis. Under the accrual basis, a company records revenue when it earns it, that is, when it delivers the goods or services. It records expenses when it incurs them. The timing of the cash does not decide the period. The cash basis records items only when cash is received or paid. The cash basis is simple, but it is not acceptable under U.S. GAAP.\nFigure F01-07 shows why the difference matters. On December 20, 2025, Orontes ships olive oil to GreenBasket Supermarkets and sends an invoice for 90. The goods cost Orontes 54. GreenBasket pays on January 15, 2026.\nFigure F01-07. Timeline of a December sale collected in January: accrual basis versus cash basis.\nUnder the accrual basis, Orontes records the revenue and the cost of goods sold in fiscal 2025, the year it delivered the goods. The gross profit of 36 belongs to 2025. In January, the cash receipt only changes one asset (the receivable) into another asset (cash). Under the cash basis, the revenue would fall in 2026, which is the wrong period.\nThe matching principle\nThe matching principle says: record expenses in the same period as the revenues they help to produce. There are three ways to apply it.\nCause and effect. Some costs link directly to a sale. Cost of goods sold is the classic example: Orontes records it at the same time as the sales revenue.\nSystematic and rational allocation. Some costs help many periods, such as a bottling line. Depreciation spreads the cost over the years of use.\nImmediate recognition. Some costs have no clear link to future revenue, such as most advertising and office salaries. They are expenses in the period in which they are incurred.\nThe accrual basis creates four common types of adjustment. You met three of them in the February transactions.\nType\nWhat happens\nOrontes example\nCreates\nPrepaid expense (deferral)\nCash is paid before the expense is incurred.\nRent paid in advance (February, row 3)\nan asset\nContract liability, or unearned revenue (deferral)\nCash is received before the revenue is earned.\nHotel pays in advance (February, row 4)\na liability\nAccrued expense\nThe expense is incurred before cash is paid.\nInterest owed on the bank note (February, row 6)\na liability\nAccrued revenue\nRevenue is earned before cash is received or billed.\nStudied in Chapter 11\nan asset\nTwo of these names are important for the exam. A prepaid expense is an asset: the company has paid for a future benefit. A contract liability (unearned revenue) is a liability: the company must still deliver the goods or services.\nEXAM TRAP   Cash is not the trigger\nCash received in advance is a liability, not revenue.\nCash paid in advance is an asset, not an expense.\nMatching means matching expenses to revenues, not to cash payments.\nTERM BRIDGE   Key words in this section\nEnglish (exam term)\nالعربية\nFrançais\naccrual basis\nأساس الاستحقاق\ncomptabilité d'engagement\ncash basis\nالأساس النقدي\ncomptabilité de caisse\nmatching principle\nمبدأ مقابلة الإيرادات بالمصروفات\nprincipe de rattachement des charges aux produits\nprepaid expense\nمصروف مدفوع مقدماً\ncharge constatée d'avance\ncontract liability (unearned revenue)\nالتزام العقد (إيراد مقبوض مقدماً)\npassif sur contrat (produit constaté d'avance)\nSECTION CHECK   Test yourself\nSC4-1  On December 28, a customer pays Orontes 60 for goods that Orontes will deliver in January. What does Orontes report at December 31?\nA.\tA liability of 60\nB.\tRevenue of 60\nC.\tAn asset of 60 and revenue of 60\nD.\tNothing until the goods are delivered\nSC4-2  Which cost is matched to revenue by cause and effect?\nA.\tDepreciation of the Amman bottling line\nB.\tAdvertising for a new product\nC.\tCost of goods sold\nD.\tRent for the head office\nAnswers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.\n1.5  Who writes the rules? U.S. GAAP and IFRS\nIn the United States, the accounting rules are called U.S. GAAP (generally accepted accounting principles). The FASB (Financial Accounting Standards Board) writes them. The FASB is a private, independent organization. It keeps all U.S. GAAP in one place: the Accounting Standards Codification (ASC). The ASC is the single source of authoritative nongovernmental U.S. GAAP. It is organized by topic numbers. For example, ASC 606 covers revenue and ASC 842 covers leases. When the FASB changes a rule, it issues an Accounting Standards Update (ASU), which amends the Codification.\nThe SEC (Securities and Exchange Commission) is a U.S. government agency. It has legal authority over the financial reporting of public companies, and it recognizes the FASB as the standard setter. For companies that report to the SEC, SEC rules are also part of authoritative GAAP. Do not confuse these bodies with the PCAOB, which oversees the audits of public companies. The PCAOB sets auditing standards, not accounting standards.\nOutside the United States, many countries use IFRS (International Financial Reporting Standards). The IASB (International Accounting Standards Board) issues them, under the IFRS Foundation. Many countries in the Middle East and North Africa, for example Jordan and the United Arab Emirates, require IFRS for listed companies. In this book, Cedar Retail S.A.L. is our IFRS company. Figure F01-08 shows both systems.\nFigure F01-08. Who writes the rules: SEC, FASB and the ASC for U.S. GAAP; IFRS Foundation and IASB for IFRS.\nThe CMA exam tests U.S. GAAP. However, Section A also asks about the main differences between U.S. GAAP and IFRS (Chapter 17). If you learned accounting under IFRS, this is good news: most concepts are the same. Every chapter has an IFRS Contrast box, so Chapter 17 will be revision for you.\nBoth systems rest on a conceptual framework: the basic ideas behind the standards, such as the users, the elements and the qualities of useful information. You studied the U.S. version in Sections 1.1 and 1.2.\n● IFRS CONTRAST   U.S. GAAP names and IFRS names\n■ U.S. GAAP (used on the exam)\n● IFRS\nbalance sheet\nstatement of financial position\nincome statement\nstatement of profit or loss\ncommon stock\nshare capital (ordinary shares)\nadditional paid-in capital\nshare premium\naccounts receivable\ntrade receivables\nnet income\nprofit\nEXAM TRAP   Four bodies, four jobs\nFASB: writes U.S. GAAP (the ASC).\nSEC: U.S. government agency with legal authority over public company reporting.\nPCAOB: sets auditing standards for audits of public companies.\nIASB: writes IFRS.\nTERM BRIDGE   Key words in this section\nEnglish (exam term)\nالعربية\nFrançais\nU.S. GAAP\nالمبادئ المحاسبية المقبولة عموماً في الولايات المتحدة\nPCGR américains (US GAAP)\nIFRS\nالمعايير الدولية لإعداد التقارير المالية\nnormes IFRS\nFASB\nمجلس معايير المحاسبة المالية\nFASB\nAccounting Standards Codification (ASC)\nتقنين معايير المحاسبة\nCodification des normes comptables (ASC)\nIASB\nمجلس معايير المحاسبة الدولية\nIASB\nSEC\nهيئة الأوراق المالية والبورصات الأمريكية\nSEC\nconceptual framework\nالإطار المفاهيمي\ncadre conceptuel\nSECTION CHECK   Test yourself\nSC5-1  Which organization writes U.S. GAAP?\nA.\tThe FASB\nB.\tThe SEC\nC.\tThe PCAOB\nD.\tThe IASB\nSC5-2  The IFRS term share premium has the same meaning as the U.S. GAAP term:\nA.\tretained earnings\nB.\tcommon stock\nC.\tnet income\nD.\tadditional paid-in capital\nAnswers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.\n1.6  A first look at the four statements\nEach statement answers a different question for users. Chapters 2 to 5 study each statement in detail. Here is the overview.\nStatement\nQuestion it answers\nTime\nChapter\nBalance sheet\nWhat does the company have and owe, and what is the owners' claim?\nat a date\n2\nIncome statement\nHow did the company perform?\nfor a period\n3\nStatement of changes in equity\nWhy did each equity account change?\nfor a period\n4\nStatement of cash flows\nWhere did cash come from, and where did it go?\nfor a period\n5\nThe four statements are linked. Figure F01-09 shows the two main links. Net income flows into retained earnings, and the net change in cash explains the cash balance on the balance sheet.\nFigure F01-09. How the four statements connect through net income, retained earnings and cash.\nYou can see both links in the January transactions of Orontes. Net income was 80: revenue of 300, minus cost of goods sold of 180, minus wages of 40. The dividend declared was 50, so retained earnings rose by 30. Cash rose by 1,060: an operating outflow of 40, an investing outflow of 1,200 and a financing inflow of 2,300.\nNotice that net income and the change in cash are very different numbers. This is the accrual basis at work.\nBefore you leave this chapter, practise reading a trial balance. A trial balance lists every account balance and checks that total debits equal total credits. Figure F01-10 is the trial balance of Barada Wholesale, Inc., a small food distributor that buys products from Orontes. You will use it in the section check below and in the practice set.\nFigure F01-10. Barada Wholesale, Inc. — trial balance, December 31, 2025 (USD 000)\nAccount\nDebit\nCredit\nCash\n1,050\n\nAccounts receivable\n300\n\nAllowance for credit losses\n\n20\nInventory\n420\n\nPrepaid rent\n24\n\nLand\n936\n\nEquipment\n4,200\n\nAccumulated depreciation\n\n900\nAccounts payable\n\n380\nDividends payable\n\n50\nNotes payable (long-term)\n\n1,800\nCommon stock\n\n600\nAdditional paid-in capital\n\n1,900\nRetained earnings, January 1\n\n1,000\nDividends declared\n50\n\nSales revenue\n\n3,100\nCost of goods sold\n1,860\n\nWages expense\n520\n\nDepreciation expense\n300\n\nInterest expense\n90\n\nTotals\n9,750\n9,750\nTERM BRIDGE   Key words in this section\nEnglish (exam term)\nالعربية\nFrançais\nbalance sheet\nالميزانية العمومية\nbilan\nstatement of financial position\nقائمة المركز المالي\nétat de la situation financière\nincome statement\nقائمة الدخل\ncompte de résultat\nstatement of changes in equity\nقائمة التغيرات في حقوق الملكية\ntableau de variation des capitaux propres\nstatement of cash flows\nقائمة التدفقات النقدية\ntableau des flux de trésorerie\nLANGUAGE FOCUS   Reading exam questions\nMOST likely or BEST describes: more than one option may be partly true. Choose the best one.\nNOT or EXCEPT: three options are true. Find the one that is false.\nimmediately after the transaction: ignore later events, such as the cash payment next month.\nnet effect: add the increases and decreases together before you answer.\nSECTION CHECK   Test yourself\nSC6-1  Which statement reports amounts at a single date?\nA.\tThe income statement\nB.\tThe balance sheet\nC.\tThe statement of changes in equity\nD.\tThe statement of cash flows\nSC6-2  Look at Figure F01-10. Which two accounts are contra-asset accounts?\nA.\tAllowance for credit losses and dividends payable\nB.\tAccumulated depreciation and notes payable\nC.\tAllowance for credit losses and accumulated depreciation\nD.\tDividends declared and accumulated depreciation\nAnswers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.\n1.7  Extended Example 1A: from a trial balance to a set of statements\nThis example takes the Barada trial balance of Figure F01-10 and builds the statements from it, in the order a company prepares them. It is exam-core: the exam often gives a trial balance and asks for one figure from one statement.\n1. The plan\nSort every account by element: asset, liability, equity, revenue or expense.\nPrepare the income statement first, because net income is needed by the next statement.\nPrepare the statement of changes in equity, which produces closing retained earnings.\nPrepare the balance sheet last, using that closing figure.\nCheck that the balance sheet balances.\n2. The income statement\nRevenue of $3,100 less cost of goods sold of $1,860 gives a gross profit of $1,240. Operating expenses are wages of $520 and depreciation of $300, so operating income is $420. Interest of $90 leaves net income of $330. Figure F01-11 sets it out.\nFigure F01-11. Extended Example 1A: the income statement from the trial balance\nBarada Wholesale, Inc. — income statement, 2025 (USD 000)\nAmount\nSales revenue\n3,100\nCost of goods sold\n(1,860)\nGross profit\n1,240\nWages expense\n(520)\nDepreciation expense\n(300)\nOperating income\n420\nInterest expense\n(90)\nNet income\n330\n3. The statement of changes in equity\nRetained earnings open at $1,000, rise by net income of $330 and fall by dividends declared of $50, closing at $1,280. Common stock of $600 and additional paid-in capital of $1,900 do not change this year, so total equity is $3,780.\n4. The balance sheet\nCurrent assets of $1,774 and non-current assets of $4,236 give total assets of $6,010. Current liabilities are $430 and the long-term note is $1,800. Figure F01-12 sets out the classified balance sheet.\nFigure F01-12. Extended Example 1A: the classified balance sheet\nBarada Wholesale, Inc. — balance sheet, December 31, 2025 (USD 000)\nAmount\nCash\n1,050\nAccounts receivable, net of the allowance\n280\nInventory\n420\nPrepaid rent\n24\nTotal current assets\n1,774\nLand\n936\nEquipment, net of accumulated depreciation\n3,300\nTotal assets\n6,010\nAccounts payable and dividends payable\n430\nNotes payable (long-term)\n1,800\nTotal liabilities\n2,230\nCommon stock and additional paid-in capital\n2,500\nRetained earnings\n1,280\nTotal equity\n3,780\nTotal liabilities and equity\n6,010\nTwo contra accounts matter here. Receivables appear net of the allowance at $280, and equipment appears net of accumulated depreciation at $3,300. Reporting either one gross would overstate assets.\n5. Tie line\nWORKED EXAMPLE   Everything ties\nAssets $6,010 = liabilities $2,230 + equity $3,780.\nOperating income $420 − interest $90 = net income $330.\nOpening retained earnings $1,000 + $330 − $50 = $1,280, the figure on the balance sheet.\n6. Reading the result\nWorking capital is $1,344 and the current ratio is 4.13, so Barada can meet its short-term obligations comfortably. A lender would also note that the long-term note of $1,800 is large against equity of $3,780.\n7. Where the fourth statement comes from\nA statement of cash flows needs last year's balance sheet as well as this one, so it cannot be built from a single trial balance. Chapter 5 prepares it for Orontes, which has both years.\n8. How the exam asks it\nEXAM TRAP   Exam view\nA trial balance question usually asks for one number: total current assets, closing retained earnings or net income. Sort the accounts by element first, and watch the contra accounts, which belong with the asset they reduce rather than with liabilities.\n9. Self-check\nSECTION CHECK   Test yourself\nSC7-1  Use Extended Example 1A. What are Barada's total current assets?\nA.\t430\nB.\t1,774\nC.\t1,794\nD.\t6,010\nSC7-2  Use Extended Example 1A. What is Barada's closing retained earnings?\nA.\t330\nB.\t1,000\nC.\t1,280\nD.\t1,330\nSC7-3  Use Extended Example 1A. Where does the dividends payable balance appear?\nA.\tIn equity, with retained earnings\nB.\tAs a contra asset\nC.\tIn non-current liabilities\nD.\tIn current liabilities\nAnswers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.\nChapter summary\nFigure F01-13 brings the chapter together. Each box now shows the key rule for its topic.\nFigure F01-13. Chapter 1 summary map: the key rule for each of the seven topics.\nGeneral-purpose financial statements serve investors, lenders and other creditors. Useful information is relevant and faithfully represented. The statements are built from elements that the accounting equation links: assets equal liabilities plus equity. Every transaction is recorded with equal debits and credits. The accrual basis records revenues when they are earned and expenses when they are incurred, and it matches expenses to the revenues they help to produce. The FASB writes U.S. GAAP in the ASC, and the IASB writes IFRS. The CMA exam uses U.S. GAAP and tests the main IFRS differences. The balance sheet shows one date, while the other three statements cover a period, and net income and cash link them.\nPractice\nAnswer the multiple-choice questions first. Then try the case question: like the exam, it gives a short case and an exhibit, followed by independent items (drag-and-drop, select from a list, fill in the blank and calculations). The answers explain why each wrong option is wrong, so read them even when you are right.\nMultiple-choice questions\nP01  Under the U.S. conceptual framework, the objective of general-purpose financial reporting is to provide useful information mainly to:\nA.\texisting and potential investors, lenders and other creditors.\nB.\tthe board of directors and senior management.\nC.\tgovernment statisticians and tax authorities.\nD.\tthe company's employees and trade unions.\nP02  A bank is deciding whether to renew a five-year loan to Orontes. Which information will the bank find MOST useful?\nA.\tThe highest and lowest share price during the year\nB.\tThe company's ability to generate cash to pay interest and repay principal\nC.\tThe number of products the company sells\nD.\tThe names of the company's major shareholders\nP03  Two companies use the same accounting methods, so an analyst can compare their results. This quality is called:\nA.\tcomparability\nB.\trelevance\nC.\tverifiability\nD.\tfaithful representation\nP04  A company issues common stock for cash. What is the effect on the accounting equation?\nA.\tAssets increase and revenue increases.\nB.\tAssets increase and liabilities increase.\nC.\tThere is no effect on the equation.\nD.\tAssets increase and equity increases.\nP05  The board declares a cash dividend that will be paid next month. What is the effect immediately after the declaration?\nA.\tExpenses increase and net income decreases.\nB.\tAssets decrease and equity decreases.\nC.\tLiabilities increase and equity decreases.\nD.\tThere is no effect until the dividend is paid.\nP06  Which transaction changes total assets but does NOT change total equity?\nA.\tPaying wages in cash\nB.\tBorrowing cash from a bank\nC.\tSelling goods on credit at a profit\nD.\tBuying equipment for cash\nP07  A company has assets of 900 and liabilities of 350. It then borrows 100 in cash and declares and pays a cash dividend of 40. What is total equity after these transactions?\nA.\t450\nB.\t510\nC.\t550\nD.\t610\nP08  Which account normally has a debit balance?\nA.\tAccounts payable\nB.\tSales revenue\nC.\tAdditional paid-in capital\nD.\tPrepaid rent\nP09  Accumulated depreciation is BEST described as:\nA.\ta contra-asset account with a credit balance.\nB.\ta liability for future asset replacement.\nC.\tan expense of the current period.\nD.\ta reduction of retained earnings.\nP10  Orontes ships goods on December 20, 2025 and invoices 90. The goods cost 54. The customer pays on January 15, 2026. The fiscal year ends on December 31. How much revenue does Orontes report for fiscal 2025?\nA.\t0\nB.\t36\nC.\t54\nD.\t90\nP11  Which statement about the matching principle is correct?\nA.\tExpenses are recognized in the period in which they are paid.\nB.\tExpenses are recognized in the same period as the revenues they help to produce.\nC.\tRevenues are recognized in the period in which cash is received.\nD.\tAll costs are capitalized as assets until related revenue is earned.\nP12  On February 10, Orontes pays 24 for 12 months of warehouse rent in advance. How does the payment appear immediately after it is made?\nA.\tAs an expense of 24\nB.\tAs a liability of 24\nC.\tIt is not recorded until the end of the month.\nD.\tAs an asset of 24\nP13  Which statement explains why retained earnings changed during the year?\nA.\tThe balance sheet\nB.\tThe statement of cash flows\nC.\tThe statement of changes in equity\nD.\tThe income statement\nP14  Net income for the period flows directly into:\nA.\tretained earnings in the statement of changes in equity.\nB.\tthe cash balance on the balance sheet.\nC.\ttotal liabilities.\nD.\tcommon stock.\nP15  Which statement is TRUE?\nA.\tThe PCAOB writes U.S. GAAP for public companies.\nB.\tThe IASB writes U.S. GAAP for companies that also report under IFRS.\nC.\tThe SEC has legal authority over public company reporting and recognizes the FASB as the standard setter.\nD.\tThe ASC contains IFRS for U.S. companies.\nP16  A Jordanian company's IFRS statements show share premium of 400. Under U.S. GAAP, the same item is called:\nA.\tretained earnings.\nB.\tcommon stock.\nC.\ttreasury stock.\nD.\tadditional paid-in capital.\nCase question: Barada Wholesale, Inc.\nBarada Wholesale, Inc. is a food wholesaler in Amman and one of Orontes's largest customers. It buys tahini, halva and oils from Orontes and sells them to shops across Jordan. Barada prepares its statements under U.S. GAAP because its bank and its main supplier ask for them.\nAt the end of 2025 Barada's bookkeeper prepared a trial balance. It lists every account with its debit or credit balance, and total debits equal total credits. The list includes cash, receivables, inventory, prepaid rent, equipment and accumulated depreciation, loans, dividends payable, revenue and several expenses.\nBarada's bank is reviewing its loan and wants three figures from the statements before it renews the credit line: net income for the year, total assets and total liabilities at the year-end. The bank also wants to know how the year's results changed retained earnings after closing.\nBarada's owner, Mr Haddad, asks you to help. He is not an accountant, so he wants each account placed in the right category before any total is calculated. Amounts in the trial balance are in thousands of U.S. dollars. He also wants to know whether dividends reduce net income, because a friend told him they do.\nUse the trial balance in Figure F01-10 for items C1-1 to C1-6.\nHow to enter answers: Enter amounts in thousands of U.S. dollars (USD 000), without the $ sign or commas (for example, 1250).\nC1-1  Match each account to its category. (On the exam screen you would drag each account into a box.)\nCategories: Asset · Contra-asset · Liability · Equity · Revenue · Expense\nAccount\nCategory\nPrepaid rent\n\nLand\n\nDividends payable\n\nAdditional paid-in capital\n\nSales revenue\n\nInterest expense\n\nAllowance for credit losses\n\nAccumulated depreciation\n\nC1-2  Enter Barada's net income for 2025 (USD 000).\nAnswer: ____________\nC1-3  Enter Barada's total assets at December 31, 2025 (USD 000).\nAnswer: ____________\nC1-4  After closing, Barada's retained earnings at December 31, 2025 are: [select]\nOptions: 330   |   1,000   |   1,280   |   1,330\nC1-5  Enter Barada's total liabilities at December 31, 2025 (USD 000).\nAnswer: ____________\nC1-6  When Barada declares a cash dividend, the dividend reduces ____; it is not an expense.\nChoose the word or phrase for the blank: retained earnings   |   net income   |   revenue   |   total assets on the declaration date\nAnswers and explanations\nSection checks\nSC1-1  Answer D. The primary users are existing and potential investors, lenders and other creditors.\nA is wrong. Managers can get internal information, so they are not primary users.\nB is wrong. Internal auditors work inside the company and use internal records.\nC is wrong. The board directs the company and can demand internal reports.\nSC1-2  Answer A. Predictive value is part of relevance: the information helps users predict the future.\nB is wrong. Verifiability means independent people could agree on the amount.\nC is wrong. Comparability means users can compare companies or periods.\nD is wrong. Timeliness means the information arrives in time to affect the decision.\nSC2-1  Answer B. Wages are a cost of the company's central operations, so they are an expense.\nA is wrong. A dividend is a distribution to owners. It reduces retained earnings but is not an expense.\nC is wrong. Repaying principal reduces a liability and an asset. No expense arises.\nD is wrong. A bottling line is an asset. Its cost becomes an expense later, through depreciation.\nSC2-2  Answer A. Equity = assets − liabilities = 500 − 320 = 180.\nB is wrong. This is the liabilities figure, not equity.\nC is wrong. This is total assets, not equity.\nD is wrong. This adds liabilities to assets. Equity is the residual after deducting liabilities.\nSC3-1  Answer B. Accumulated depreciation is a contra-asset account, so it has the opposite (credit) balance to assets.\nA is wrong. Prepaid rent is an asset, so its normal balance is a debit.\nC is wrong. Cost of goods sold is an expense, so its normal balance is a debit.\nD is wrong. Dividends declared reduce equity, so they have a debit balance.\nSC3-2  Answer D. One asset increases (debit Equipment) and another asset decreases (credit Cash).\nA is wrong. The sides are reversed: this entry would increase cash and decrease equipment.\nB is wrong. The bottling line is an asset, not an expense of this period.\nC is wrong. Orontes paid cash; it did not borrow. The credit side must be Cash.\nSC4-1  Answer A. Cash increases, and Orontes owes the customer the goods. This is a contract liability (unearned revenue).\nB is wrong. Revenue is not earned until the goods are delivered in January.\nC is wrong. The cash asset is correct, but the credit is a liability, not revenue.\nD is wrong. Cash has been received, so Orontes must record it now.\nSC4-2  Answer C. Cost of goods sold links directly to each sale, so it is recorded with the sales revenue.\nA is wrong. Depreciation uses systematic and rational allocation over the years of use.\nB is wrong. Advertising has no clear link to specific revenue, so it is recognized immediately.\nD is wrong. Head-office rent is a period cost, recognized immediately.\nSC5-1  Answer A. The FASB writes U.S. GAAP and keeps it in the Accounting Standards Codification.\nB is wrong. The SEC has legal authority, but it recognizes the FASB as the standard setter.\nC is wrong. The PCAOB sets auditing standards, not accounting standards.\nD is wrong. The IASB writes IFRS, not U.S. GAAP.\nSC5-2  Answer D. Share premium is the amount received for shares above their par value: additional paid-in capital.\nA is wrong. Retained earnings come from past profits, not from selling shares.\nB is wrong. Common stock holds only the par value of the shares.\nC is wrong. Net income is a performance measure, not an equity account.\nSC6-1  Answer B. The balance sheet shows position at one date. The other statements cover a period.\nA is wrong. The income statement covers a period.\nC is wrong. The statement of changes in equity covers a period.\nD is wrong. The statement of cash flows covers a period.\nSC6-2  Answer C. Both accounts reduce an asset (receivables and equipment) and have credit balances.\nA is wrong. Dividends payable is a liability, not a contra-asset.\nB is wrong. Notes payable is a liability, not a contra-asset.\nD is wrong. Dividends declared reduces equity, not an asset.\nSC7-1  Answer B. Cash, receivables net of the allowance, inventory and prepaid rent.\nA is wrong. This is total current liabilities.\nC is wrong. This adds the allowance back instead of subtracting it.\nD is wrong. This is total assets, including land and equipment.\nSC7-2  Answer C. Opening retained earnings plus net income less dividends declared.\nA is wrong. This is net income, not the retained earnings balance.\nB is wrong. This is the opening balance, before net income.\nD is wrong. This ignores the dividend declared.\nSC7-3  Answer D. It is an obligation to pay cash within a year.\nA is wrong. A declared dividend is a liability, not part of equity.\nB is wrong. It reduces nothing on the asset side.\nC is wrong. It is payable within the year.\nYour turn: February, rows 4 to 6\nRow\nTransaction\nJournal entry\nEffects\n4\nA Dubai hotel group paid cash in advance for pastries that Orontes will deliver in March.\nDr Cash 60; Cr Contract liability (unearned revenue) 60\nAssets +60, liabilities +60, equity 0, net income 0; cash flow: operating +60.\n5\nPaid the dividend declared on January 31.\nDr Dividends payable 50; Cr Cash 50\nAssets (50), liabilities (50), equity 0, net income 0; cash flow: financing (50).\n6\nRecorded one month of interest on the bank note. Orontes has not paid it yet.\nDr Interest expense 4; Cr Interest payable 4\nAssets 0, liabilities +4, equity (4), net income (4); cash flow: none.\nRow 4 is a liability, not revenue: Orontes must still deliver the pastries. Row 5 reduces a liability and cash; retained earnings fell in January, when the dividend was declared. Row 6 is an accrued expense: interest is an expense of February even though Orontes has not paid it.\nMultiple-choice questions\nP01  Answer A. These are the primary users. They provide resources and cannot demand special reports.\nB is wrong. Management can obtain internal information, so it is not a primary user.\nC is wrong. Governments use the statements, but they are not the primary users named by the framework.\nD is wrong. Employees are users, but not the primary users named by the framework.\nP02  Answer B. A lender's main question is repayment: can the borrower pay interest and principal on time?\nA is wrong. Share prices interest investors more than lenders and do not show repayment ability.\nC is wrong. The product count does not show whether the company can repay the loan.\nD is wrong. Ownership details do not answer the lender's repayment question.\nP03  Answer A. Comparability lets users identify similarities and differences between companies or periods.\nB is wrong. Relevance means the information can make a difference to a decision.\nC is wrong. Verifiability means independent observers could reach the same result.\nD is wrong. Faithful representation means complete, neutral and free from error.\nP04  Answer D. Cash (an asset) increases, and contributed capital (equity) increases by the same amount.\nA is wrong. Selling shares is financing from owners, not revenue.\nB is wrong. Shares are equity, not debt. A liability arises only when the company borrows.\nC is wrong. Both sides of the equation increase, so the equation changes but still balances.\nP05  Answer C. Declaring the dividend creates dividends payable (a liability) and reduces retained earnings.\nA is wrong. A dividend is a distribution to owners, not an expense.\nB is wrong. Cash decreases only on the payment date, next month.\nD is wrong. The obligation exists from the declaration date, so it is recorded now.\nP06  Answer B. Assets and liabilities both increase. Equity does not change.\nA is wrong. Wages are an expense, so equity (retained earnings) also decreases.\nC is wrong. A profitable sale increases equity through net income.\nD is wrong. One asset replaces another, so total assets do not change.\nP07  Answer B. Equity starts at 550 (900 − 350). Borrowing does not change equity. The dividend reduces it by 40, so equity is 510.\nA is wrong. This is total liabilities after the loan (350 + 100), not equity.\nC is wrong. This ignores the dividend, which reduces retained earnings.\nD is wrong. This adds the loan to equity. A loan is a liability.\nP08  Answer D. Prepaid rent is an asset, and assets have debit balances.\nA is wrong. Accounts payable is a liability, with a credit balance.\nB is wrong. Revenue increases equity, so it has a credit balance.\nC is wrong. APIC is equity, with a credit balance.\nP09  Answer A. It reduces the cost of equipment on the balance sheet and has a credit balance.\nB is wrong. The company owes nobody anything, so it is not a liability.\nC is wrong. Depreciation expense is the period's charge; accumulated depreciation is the running total on the balance sheet.\nD is wrong. It reduces an asset, not equity directly.\nP10  Answer D. Under the accrual basis, revenue is recorded when the goods are delivered, in 2025.\nA is wrong. This is cash-basis thinking. The cash timing does not decide the period.\nB is wrong. This is the gross profit (revenue minus cost), not the revenue.\nC is wrong. This is the cost of goods sold, not the revenue.\nP11  Answer B. This is the definition of matching.\nA is wrong. Payment timing does not decide when an expense is recognized.\nC is wrong. Revenue is recognized when earned, not when cash is received.\nD is wrong. Many costs (for example, advertising) are expensed immediately.\nP12  Answer D. Prepaid rent is an asset: Orontes has paid for 12 months of future use.\nA is wrong. The benefit is in the future, so the cost becomes an expense month by month.\nB is wrong. This confuses a prepaid expense (asset) with unearned revenue (liability).\nC is wrong. Cash has left the company, so the transaction must be recorded now.\nP13  Answer C. The statement of changes in equity shows beginning retained earnings, net income, dividends and ending retained earnings.\nA is wrong. The balance sheet shows only the ending balance at one date.\nB is wrong. The statement of cash flows explains cash, not equity.\nD is wrong. The income statement shows net income but not dividends or the opening balance.\nP14  Answer A. Net income is closed into retained earnings.\nB is wrong. Net income is not cash. The statement of cash flows explains the cash balance.\nC is wrong. Net income belongs to the owners, so it goes to equity, not liabilities.\nD is wrong. Common stock changes only when shares are issued or retired.\nP15  Answer C. The SEC has the legal authority; the FASB sets the standards; the ASC holds U.S. GAAP.\nA is wrong. The PCAOB sets auditing standards.\nB is wrong. The IASB writes IFRS only.\nD is wrong. The ASC is U.S. GAAP. IFRS is a separate set of standards.\nP16  Answer D. Share premium and additional paid-in capital both mean the amount received above par value.\nA is wrong. Retained earnings come from profits, not from issuing shares.\nB is wrong. Common stock holds only the par value.\nC is wrong. Treasury stock is the company's own shares that it bought back (Chapter 4).\nCase question: Barada Wholesale, Inc.\nC1-1\nAccount\nCategory\nPrepaid rent\nAsset\nLand\nAsset\nDividends payable\nLiability\nAdditional paid-in capital\nEquity\nSales revenue\nRevenue\nInterest expense\nExpense\nAllowance for credit losses\nContra-asset\nAccumulated depreciation\nContra-asset\nWatch the two contra-asset accounts: they sit with the assets but have credit balances. Dividends payable is a liability; dividends declared (in the trial balance) reduce equity.\nC1-2  Answer: 330.\n280 is wrong: this answer subtracts the dividends declared as if they were an expense.\n1,240 is wrong: this answer stops at gross profit (revenue minus cost of goods sold).\n3,100 is wrong: this answer reports revenue instead of net income.\nC1-3  Answer: 6,010.\n6,030 is wrong: this answer ignores the allowance for credit losses.\n6,910 is wrong: this answer ignores accumulated depreciation.\n7,850 is wrong: this answer adds the contra-asset balances instead of subtracting them.\nC1-4  Answer: 1,280.\n1,330 is wrong: this answer forgets to subtract the dividends declared.\n330 is wrong: this answer reports only this year's net income.\nC1-5  Answer: 2,230.\n2,280 is wrong: this answer also counts dividends declared (an equity item) as a liability.\n2,180 is wrong: this answer leaves out dividends payable, which is a liability.\nC1-6  Answer: retained earnings.\nnet income is wrong: this answer treats a dividend as an expense that reduces net income.\nrevenue is wrong: this answer a dividend is a distribution, not a reduction of revenue.\ntotal assets on the declaration date is wrong: this answer cash leaves only when the dividend is paid; declaration creates a liability.\nPART I — FOUNDATIONS AND THE FOUR FINANCIAL STATEMENTS\nChapter 2";
